// Rep Max questions: Mathematics (Pearson Edexcel 1MA1), GCSE. One question per line, no commas between lines.
// Fields: id (unique), s (subject), t (topic id from spec.json), lv (1 warm-up, 2 working set, 3 hardest; Higher-only topics are 3),
// q (question; maths between $ signs, backslashes doubled), code (optional), o (four options), a (index 0-3 of the right one), x (explanation).
// Written by AI (Sonnet) and checked independently by AI (Opus), 15 Nov 2026; logs in the library's provenance folder.
{"id":"gma-001","s":"maths","t":"N1-N3","lv":1,"q":"Work out $3 + 4 \\times 2^2 - 5$.","o":["$23$","$14$","$62$","$116$"],"a":1,"x":"Powers first: $2^2=4$. Then multiply: $4\\times4=16$. Then $3+16-5=14$.","w":["Did $3+4$ first, then multiplied by $2^2$; BIDMAS says multiply before adding.","","Squared $4\\times2$ to get $64$; the power only applies to the $2$.","Squared all of $3+4\\times2$; the power only applies to the $2$."]}
{"id":"gma-002","s":"maths","t":"N1-N3","lv":2,"q":"Work out $2\\frac{1}{3} + 1\\frac{3}{4}$.","o":["$4\\frac{4}{7}$","$3\\frac{1}{12}$","$4\\frac{1}{7}$","$4\\frac{1}{12}$"],"a":3,"x":"Add whole numbers: 3. Then $\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{4}=\\frac{4}{12}+\\frac{9}{12}=\\frac{13}{12}=1\\frac{1}{12}$. Total $4\\frac{1}{12}$.","w":["$\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{3}{4}$ is not $\\frac{4}{7}$; use twelfths: $\\frac{13}{12}$.","Dropped the extra whole from $\\frac{13}{12}$; it makes $1\\frac{1}{12}$, so add that 1.","The fractions make $1\\frac{1}{12}$, not $1\\frac{1}{7}$; use twelfths.",""]}
{"id":"gma-003","s":"maths","t":"N4-N5","lv":1,"q":"What is the highest common factor (HCF) of 24 and 36?","o":["$6$","$72$","$4$","$12$"],"a":3,"x":"Factors of 24 and 36 shared: 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 12. The highest is 12. (72 is the LCM.)","w":["6 is a common factor but not the highest; 12 also divides both.","72 is the LCM (lowest common multiple), not the HCF.","4 is a common factor but not the highest; 12 is bigger and shared.",""]}
{"id":"gma-004","s":"maths","t":"N6-N7","lv":1,"q":"Work out the value of $2^3 \\times 2^4$.","o":["$4096$","$56$","$128$","$14$"],"a":2,"x":"Add the powers when multiplying: $2^3\\times2^4=2^7=128$.","w":["Multiplied the powers ($3\\times4=12$); you add them when multiplying.","Did $2^3=8$, then $8\\times7$; just add the powers to get $2^7$.","","Multiplied the base by the powers' sum; the base stays 2, giving $2^7$."]}
{"id":"gma-005","s":"maths","t":"N8","lv":1,"q":"A circle has radius 3 cm. What is its area, in terms of $\\pi$?","o":["$9\\pi$ cm²","$6\\pi$ cm²","$18\\pi$ cm²","$3\\pi$ cm²"],"a":0,"x":"Area $=\\pi r^2=\\pi\\times3^2=9\\pi$. ($6\\pi$ is the circumference.)","w":["","Used $2\\pi r$, which is the circumference, not the area.","Doubled $\\pi r^2$; the area is $\\pi\\times3^2$, not twice that.","Used $\\pi r$; you must square the radius for the area."]}
{"id":"gma-006","s":"maths","t":"N9","lv":1,"q":"Write $0.00045$ in standard form.","o":["$4.5\\times10^{-3}$","$45\\times10^{-5}$","$4.5\\times10^{4}$","$4.5\\times10^{-4}$"],"a":3,"x":"Move the decimal point 4 places right to get $4.5$, so the power is $-4$. ($45\\times10^{-5}$ is not standard form.)","w":["Counted only 3 places; the point moves 4 places to get $4.5$.","$45$ is not between 1 and 10, so this isn't standard form.","Positive power; numbers below 1 need a negative power.",""]}
{"id":"gma-007","s":"maths","t":"N10-N12","lv":1,"q":"Write $0.375$ as a fraction in its simplest form.","o":["$\\frac{3}{8}$","$\\frac{3}{10}$","$\\frac{5}{8}$","$\\frac{37}{100}$"],"a":0,"x":"$0.375=\\frac{375}{1000}$. Divide top and bottom by 125 to get $\\frac{3}{8}$.","w":["","Read $0.375$ as $\\frac{3}{10}$; the digits 75 matter too, as it's thousandths.","$\\frac{5}{8}=0.625$; $0.375$ is $\\frac{3}{8}$.","Dropped the last digit and used hundredths; $0.375$ is in thousandths."]}
{"id":"gma-008","s":"maths","t":"N13-N16","lv":2,"q":"A number $x$ is rounded to 1 decimal place. The result is $7.4$. What is the error interval for $x$?","o":["$7.35 \\le x \\le 7.45$","$7.4 \\le x < 7.5$","$7.3 \\le x < 7.5$","$7.35 \\le x < 7.45$"],"a":3,"x":"Half a unit of the last place is $0.05$. So $7.35$ up to but not including $7.45$, since $7.45$ rounds up to $7.5$.","w":["Includes $7.45$, but $7.45$ rounds up to $7.5$, so use $<$ at the top.","Started at $7.4$ itself; the interval goes down half a unit to $7.35$.","Used a whole unit of $0.1$ each way instead of half a unit, $0.05$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-009","s":"maths","t":"A1-A3","lv":1,"q":"Work out the value of $2x^2 - x$ when $x = -3$.","o":["$-15$","$39$","$21$","$-21$"],"a":2,"x":"Square first: $(-3)^2=9$, so $2\\times9=18$. Then $18-(-3)=21$.","w":["Treated $-3^2$ as $-9$; the bracket makes $(-3)^2=9$.","Squared $2x$ to get $36$; only $x$ is squared, not the 2.","","Used $-9$ for $(-3)^2$ and $-3$ for $-(-3)$; both should be positive."]}
{"id":"gma-010","s":"maths","t":"A4","lv":1,"q":"Expand and simplify $(x+3)(x-5)$.","o":["$x^2+2x-15$","$x^2-2x+15$","$x^2-2x-15$","$x^2-8x-15$"],"a":2,"x":"$x^2-5x+3x-15=x^2-2x-15$.","w":["Middle term sign wrong: $-5x+3x=-2x$, not $+2x$.","Constant should be $3\\times(-5)=-15$, not $+15$.","","Combined $-5x$ and $3x$ as $-8x$; they combine to $-2x$."]}
{"id":"gma-011","s":"maths","t":"A4","lv":2,"q":"Factorise $x^2-5x-14$.","o":["$(x-7)(x+2)$","$(x+7)(x-2)$","$(x-7)(x-2)$","$(x+7)(x+2)$"],"a":0,"x":"Find two numbers with product $-14$ and sum $-5$: $-7$ and $+2$.","w":["","Signs swapped; this expands to $x^2+5x-14$, not $-5x$.","Both negative gives $+14$ as the constant, not $-14$.","Both positive gives $+14$ and $+9x$, not $-14$ and $-5x$."]}
{"id":"gma-012","s":"maths","t":"A5","lv":2,"q":"Make $x$ the subject of $y=\\frac{x+4}{3}$.","o":["$x=3y-4$","$x=3y+4$","$x=\\frac{y}{3}-4$","$x=\\frac{y-4}{3}$"],"a":0,"x":"Multiply both sides by 3: $3y=x+4$. Then subtract 4: $x=3y-4$.","w":["","Sign slip: $x+4=3y$ means subtract 4, not add 4.","Divided $y$ by 3 instead of multiplying; undo $\\div3$ by $\\times3$.","Divided by 3 again; undo $\\div3$ by multiplying by 3, then subtract 4."]}
{"id":"gma-013","s":"maths","t":"A6-A7","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is an identity (true for every value of $x$)?","o":["$(x+3)^2=x^2+9$","$2x+1=x+4$","$3(x+2)=3x+6$","$x^2=4x$"],"a":2,"x":"Expanding $3(x+2)$ gives $3x+6$ for every $x$, so we can write $3(x+2)\\equiv3x+6$. $(x+3)^2=x^2+6x+9$, and the other two are true only for some values of $x$.","w":["Forgot the middle term: $(x+3)^2=x^2+6x+9$, so this fails.","Only true when $x=3$, not for every $x$.","","Only true for $x=0$ and $x=4$, not for every $x$."]}
{"id":"gma-014","s":"maths","t":"A8-A10","lv":2,"q":"A line is parallel to $y=2x+5$ and passes through $(1, 3)$. What is its equation?","o":["$y=2x+1$","$y=2x+3$","$y=-2x+5$","$y=2x-1$"],"a":0,"x":"Parallel lines have gradient 2. Use $(1,3)$: $3=2\\times1+c$, so $c=1$.","w":["","Used the $y$-value 3 as the intercept; $c$ must come from substituting $(1,3)$.","Gradient is $-2$, but parallel lines have the same gradient, $2$.","Sign slip: $3=2+c$ gives $c=1$, not $-1$."]}
{"id":"gma-015","s":"maths","t":"A11-A12","lv":2,"q":"Where does the graph of $y=x^2-6x+5$ cross the $x$-axis?","o":["$x=-1$ and $x=-5$","$x=1$ and $x=5$","$x=-1$ and $x=5$","$x=0$ and $x=6$"],"a":1,"x":"Put $y=0$: $x^2-6x+5=(x-1)(x-5)=0$, so $x=1$ or $x=5$.","w":["Sign error: the factors $(x-1)(x-5)$ give positive roots.","","One sign wrong: $(x-1)(x-5)=0$ gives $x=1$, not $x=-1$.","Factorised $x^2-6x$ and ignored the $+5$; set $y=0$ for the full quadratic."]}
{"id":"gma-016","s":"maths","t":"A13","lv":3,"q":"The graph of $y=f(x)$ has a minimum point at $(2,-1)$. Where is the minimum of $y=f(x-1)+4$?","o":["$(1, 3)$","$(3, 3)$","$(3, -5)$","$(1, -5)$"],"a":1,"x":"$f(x-1)$ moves the graph 1 to the right: $x=3$. The $+4$ moves it up 4: $y=-1+4=3$.","w":["Moved left 1, not right 1: $f(x-1)$ shifts right.","","Moved right correctly but went down 4; $+4$ shifts up.","Both shifts in the wrong direction."]}
{"id":"gma-017","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":1,"q":"On a distance-time graph, a horizontal line shows that the object is:","o":["not moving","moving at a steady speed","speeding up","returning to the start"],"a":0,"x":"Distance is not changing as time passes, so the object is stationary.","w":["","A steady speed is a sloping straight line, not a flat one.","Speeding up is a curve getting steeper; a flat line has no slope.","Returning to the start is a line sloping downwards."]}
{"id":"gma-018","s":"maths","t":"A16","lv":3,"q":"The point $(3,4)$ is on the circle $x^2+y^2=25$. What is the equation of the tangent to the circle at this point?","o":["$4x+3y=25$","$3x+4y=25$","$3x-4y=-7$","$4x-3y=0$"],"a":1,"x":"Radius gradient is $\\frac{4}{3}$, so the tangent gradient is $-\\frac{3}{4}$. $y-4=-\\frac{3}{4}(x-3)$ gives $3x+4y=25$.","w":["Swapped the coefficients; $4x+3y=25$ gives 24 at $(3,4)$, so misses the point.","","Passes through $(3,4)$ but gradient is $\\frac{3}{4}$, not the perpendicular $-\\frac{3}{4}$.","This is the radius line through the origin, not the tangent."]}
{"id":"gma-019","s":"maths","t":"A17-A18","lv":1,"q":"Solve $3x+7=22$.","o":["$x=\\frac{29}{3}$","$x=15$","$x=5$","$x=-5$"],"a":2,"x":"Subtract 7: $3x=15$. Divide by 3: $x=5$.","w":["Added 7 instead of subtracting it before dividing by 3.","Subtracted 7 but forgot to divide by 3.","","Sign slip: $3\\times(-5)+7=-8$, not 22."]}
{"id":"gma-020","s":"maths","t":"A17-A18","lv":2,"q":"Solve $x^2-3x-10=0$.","o":["$x=5$ or $x=-2$","$x=-5$ or $x=2$","$x=10$ or $x=-1$","$x=5$ or $x=2$"],"a":0,"x":"$(x-5)(x+2)=0$, so $x=5$ or $x=-2$.","w":["","Signs swapped: $(x+5)(x-2)$ expands to $x^2+3x-10$.","10 and $-1$ multiply to $-10$ but add to 9, not $-3$.","Both roots positive would give $+10$, not $-10$, as the constant."]}
{"id":"gma-021","s":"maths","t":"A19","lv":2,"q":"Solve $2x+y=11$ and $x-y=1$.","o":["$x=3,\\ y=4$","$x=4,\\ y=3$","$x=5,\\ y=1$","$x=6,\\ y=-1$"],"a":1,"x":"Add the equations: $3x=12$, so $x=4$. Then $4-y=1$ gives $y=3$.","w":["Swapped $x$ and $y$; check $x-y=1$: $3-4=-1$.","","Fits the first equation only; $5-1=4$, not 1.","Fits the first equation only; $6-(-1)=7$, not 1."]}
{"id":"gma-022","s":"maths","t":"A20","lv":3,"q":"$x_{n+1}=\\sqrt{x_n+5}$ and $x_0=1$. Use a calculator to work out $x_2$, to 2 decimal places.","o":["$2.45$","$2.78$","$6.57$","$2.73$"],"a":3,"x":"$x_1=\\sqrt{1+5}=2.449\\ldots$ Then $x_2=\\sqrt{2.449\\ldots+5}=2.729\\ldots\\approx2.73$.","w":["That is $x_1$; one more step is needed to reach $x_2$.","That is $x_3$; you did one step too many.","Too big: $x_2=\\sqrt{x_1+5}$ and $x_1+5<9$, so $x_2<3$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-023","s":"maths","t":"A21-A22","lv":2,"q":"Solve $5-2x \\le 11$.","o":["$x \\le -3$","$x \\ge 3$","$x \\ge -3$","$x \\le 3$"],"a":2,"x":"Subtract 5: $-2x\\le6$. Dividing by a negative number flips the sign: $x\\ge-3$.","w":["Divided by $-2$ but did not flip the inequality sign.","Flipped the sign correctly, but $6\\div(-2)=-3$, not 3.","","Two slips: $6\\div(-2)=-3$, and the sign must flip to $\\ge$."]}
{"id":"gma-024","s":"maths","t":"A23-A25","lv":1,"q":"Find the $n$th term of the sequence 5, 8, 11, 14, ...","o":["$3n+2$","$3n+5$","$5n+3$","$n+3$"],"a":0,"x":"The difference is 3, so start with $3n$. When $n=1$, $3n=3$; add 2 to get 5.","w":["","Used the first term 5 as the constant; $3+5=8$ not 5 at $n=1$.","Mixed up the difference and the first term.","Used $n+3$, but the difference is 3, so $n$ needs coefficient 3."]}
{"id":"gma-025","s":"maths","t":"R1-R2","lv":2,"q":"On a map with scale 1 : 50 000, two towns are 4 cm apart. What is the real distance?","o":["20 km","2 km","200 m","0.2 km"],"a":1,"x":"$4\\times50\\,000=200\\,000$ cm. Divide by 100 for metres: 2000 m, which is 2 km.","w":["Decimal slip: 200 000 cm is 2 km, not 20 km.","","Divided by 1000, not 100: 200 000 cm is 2000 m, not 200 m.","Decimal slip: 200 000 cm is 2 km, not 0.2 km."]}
{"id":"gma-026","s":"maths","t":"R3-R8","lv":1,"q":"Write the ratio $18:24$ in its simplest form.","o":["$9:12$","$2:3$","$4:3$","$3:4$"],"a":3,"x":"Divide both numbers by their HCF, 6: $18\\div6=3$ and $24\\div6=4$.","w":["Only divided by 2; both numbers still share a factor of 3.","Not equal to $18:24$; $18\\div6=3$, not 2.","Ratio written the wrong way round, and 18 does not become 4.",""]}
{"id":"gma-027","s":"maths","t":"R9","lv":2,"q":"After a 20% increase, a price is £90. What was the price before the increase?","o":["£72","£70","£108","£75"],"a":3,"x":"£90 is 120% of the original. Divide by 1.2: $90\\div1.2=75$.","w":["Took 20% off £90, but 20% was added to the original price, not to £90.","Subtracted £20 instead of dividing by 1.2.","Multiplied by 1.2 (a further increase) instead of dividing.",""]}
{"id":"gma-028","s":"maths","t":"R10-R11","lv":2,"q":"A metal block has mass 540 g and volume 200 cm³. What is its density?","o":["2.7 g/cm³","0.37 g/cm³","340 g/cm³","27 g/cm³"],"a":0,"x":"Density $=\\text{mass}\\div\\text{volume}=540\\div200=2.7$ g/cm³.","w":["","Divided volume by mass, the wrong way round.","Subtracted the numbers instead of dividing mass by volume.","Decimal slip: $540\\div200$ is 2.7, not 27."]}
{"id":"gma-029","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":3,"q":"Two similar solids have lengths in the ratio $2:5$. The smaller has volume 40 cm³. What is the volume of the larger?","o":["250 cm³","100 cm³","625 cm³","1000 cm³"],"a":2,"x":"Volume scale factor is the cube of the length factor: $(\\frac{5}{2})^3=15.625$. Then $40\\times15.625=625$.","w":["Used the area factor $(5/2)^2=6.25$ instead of cubing.","Used the length factor 2.5 only; volume needs the cube.","","Squared 5 for 25 and multiplied; need $(5/2)^3$ for volume."]}
{"id":"gma-030","s":"maths","t":"R15","lv":3,"q":"A tangent is drawn at $t=3$ s on a distance-time graph. It passes through $(1, 2)$ and $(5, 14)$ (distance in m). What is the speed at $t=3$?","o":["$\\frac{1}{3}$ m/s","2.8 m/s","12 m/s","3 m/s"],"a":3,"x":"The speed is the gradient of the tangent: $\\frac{14-2}{5-1}=3$ m/s.","w":["Inverted the gradient: it is change in distance over change in time.","Divided 14 by 5, using one point instead of the gradient between two.","Only found the rise (14 − 2); you must divide by the run too.",""]}
{"id":"gma-031","s":"maths","t":"R16","lv":2,"q":"£2000 is invested at 4% compound interest per year. How much is it worth after 3 years?","o":["£2240.00","£2249.73","£2163.20","£2339.72"],"a":1,"x":"Multiply by 1.04 three times: $2000\\times1.04^3=2249.73$. (£2240 would be simple interest.)","w":["That is simple interest: 4% of £2000 each year, with no interest on interest.","","Only two years of compounding: $2000\\times1.04^2$ is £2163.20.","Four years of compounding instead of three."]}
{"id":"gma-032","s":"maths","t":"G1-G2","lv":1,"q":"Which locus is the set of points equidistant from two fixed points $A$ and $B$?","o":["The perpendicular bisector of $AB$","The bisector of an angle at $A$","A circle with centre $A$","A line parallel to $AB$"],"a":0,"x":"Points the same distance from $A$ and $B$ lie on the line that cuts $AB$ in half at right angles.","w":["","That is the locus equidistant from two lines, not two points.","That is all points a fixed distance from $A$ only.","Parallel lines stay a fixed distance from $AB$, not equal from $A$ and $B$."]}
{"id":"gma-033","s":"maths","t":"G3-G4","lv":1,"q":"What is the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon?","o":["$540^\\circ$","$1080^\\circ$","$720^\\circ$","$360^\\circ$"],"a":2,"x":"Sum $=(n-2)\\times180=4\\times180=720^\\circ$.","w":["That is a pentagon: $(5-2)\\times180$. A hexagon has 6 sides.","That is an octagon: $(8-2)\\times180$.","","That is the sum of the exterior angles, not the interior angles."]}
{"id":"gma-034","s":"maths","t":"G3-G4","lv":2,"q":"Each exterior angle of a regular polygon is $18^\\circ$. How many sides does it have?","o":["$10$","$18$","$22$","$20$"],"a":3,"x":"Exterior angles add up to $360^\\circ$. $360\\div18=20$ sides.","w":["Divided 180 by 18; exterior angles add to 360°, not 180°.","Mixed up the angle with the number of sides.","Slip in the division: $360\\div18$ is exactly 20.",""]}
{"id":"gma-035","s":"maths","t":"G5-G6","lv":1,"q":"A right-angled triangle has shorter sides 6 cm and 8 cm. How long is the hypotenuse?","o":["14 cm","100 cm","10 cm","5.3 cm"],"a":2,"x":"$6^2+8^2=36+64=100$. The hypotenuse is $\\sqrt{100}=10$ cm.","w":["Added 6 and 8 instead of using Pythagoras.","Forgot the square root: 100 is $c^2$, not $c$.","","Subtracted the squares ($64-36$); the hypotenuse needs adding."]}
{"id":"gma-036","s":"maths","t":"G7-G8","lv":2,"q":"The point $(2,1)$ is rotated $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise about the origin. What are its new coordinates?","o":["$(1, -2)$","$(-2, -1)$","$(-1, 2)$","$(2, -1)$"],"a":2,"x":"A $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise turn takes $(x,y)$ to $(-y,x)$. So $(2,1)\\to(-1,2)$.","w":["That is $90^\\circ$ clockwise, not anticlockwise.","That is a $180^\\circ$ rotation, not $90^\\circ$.","","That is a reflection in the x-axis, not a rotation."]}
{"id":"gma-037","s":"maths","t":"G9-G10","lv":3,"q":"$AB$ is a diameter of a circle and $C$ is a point on the circumference. Angle $CAB=34^\\circ$. What is angle $ABC$?","o":["$73^\\circ$","$34^\\circ$","$146^\\circ$","$56^\\circ$"],"a":3,"x":"Angle $ACB=90^\\circ$ (the angle in a semicircle is a right angle). Angles in a triangle add to $180^\\circ$, so angle $ABC=180-90-34=56^\\circ$.","w":["Half of $146^\\circ$: forgot the right angle and halved $180-34$.","Copied the given angle; the other acute angle is not equal to it.","Did $180-34$ and forgot the $90^\\circ$ angle in a semicircle.",""]}
{"id":"gma-038","s":"maths","t":"G11-G15","lv":2,"q":"The bearing of $B$ from $A$ is $065^\\circ$. What is the bearing of $A$ from $B$?","o":["$115^\\circ$","$245^\\circ$","$025^\\circ$","$295^\\circ$"],"a":1,"x":"Back bearings differ by $180^\\circ$: $65+180=245^\\circ$.","w":["Did $180-65$ instead of adding $180^\\circ$.","","Used $90-65$; a back bearing needs $180^\\circ$ added.","Did $360-65$, which is not the reverse direction."]}
{"id":"gma-039","s":"maths","t":"G16-G18","lv":2,"q":"A sector has radius 6 cm and angle $60^\\circ$. What is its area, in terms of $\\pi$?","o":["$12\\pi$ cm²","$36\\pi$ cm²","$6\\pi$ cm²","$2\\pi$ cm²"],"a":2,"x":"Area $=\\frac{60}{360}\\times\\pi\\times6^2=\\frac{1}{6}\\times36\\pi=6\\pi$.","w":["That is the full circumference $2\\pi r$, not a sector area.","That is the whole circle's area; forgot the $\\frac{60}{360}$ fraction.","","That is the arc length, not the area."]}
{"id":"gma-040","s":"maths","t":"G16-G18","lv":2,"q":"A cone has base radius 6 cm and vertical height 8 cm. Curved surface area $=\\pi rl$, where $l$ is the slant height. What is its curved surface area, in terms of $\\pi$?","o":["$48\\pi$ cm²","$60\\pi$ cm²","$96\\pi$ cm²","$120\\pi$ cm²"],"a":1,"x":"Slant height $=\\sqrt{6^2+8^2}=10$. Curved area $=\\pi rl=\\pi\\times6\\times10=60\\pi$.","w":["Used the vertical height 8 as the slant height instead of 10.","","That includes the base ($60\\pi+36\\pi$), but the question asks for curved area only.","Doubled $\\pi rl$, as if using $2\\pi rl$."]}
{"id":"gma-041","s":"maths","t":"G19-G21","lv":2,"q":"In a right-angled triangle, angle $x$ has opposite side 6 cm and adjacent side 8 cm. Use a calculator to find $x$ to 1 decimal place.","o":["$36.9^\\circ$","$53.1^\\circ$","$48.6^\\circ$","$41.4^\\circ$"],"a":0,"x":"$\\tan x=\\frac{6}{8}$, so $x=\\tan^{-1}(0.75)=36.9^\\circ$.","w":["","Inverted the ratio: $\\tan^{-1}(\\frac{8}{6})$, not $\\frac{6}{8}$.","Used $\\sin^{-1}(0.75)$, but the sides are opposite and adjacent, so tan.","Used $\\cos^{-1}(0.75)$; the sides are opposite and adjacent, so use tan."]}
{"id":"gma-042","s":"maths","t":"G22-G23","lv":3,"q":"A triangle has sides 8 cm and 5 cm with an angle of $30^\\circ$ between them. What is its area?","o":["20 cm²","17.3 cm²","34.6 cm²","10 cm²"],"a":3,"x":"Area $=\\frac{1}{2}ab\\sin C=\\frac{1}{2}\\times8\\times5\\times\\sin30^\\circ=20\\times0.5=10$.","w":["Forgot to multiply by $\\sin30^\\circ$ (just $\\frac{1}{2}\\times8\\times5$).","Used $\\sin60^\\circ$ instead of $\\sin30^\\circ$.","Used $ab\\sin60^\\circ$: wrong angle and no $\\frac{1}{2}$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-043","s":"maths","t":"G24-G25","lv":2,"q":"$\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\-2\\end{pmatrix}$ and $\\mathbf{b}=\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}$. Work out $2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}$.","o":["$\\begin{pmatrix}7\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}5\\\\-8\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}1\\\\-10\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}5\\\\0\\end{pmatrix}$"],"a":1,"x":"$2\\mathbf{a}=\\begin{pmatrix}6\\\\-4\\end{pmatrix}$. Subtract $\\mathbf{b}$: $\\begin{pmatrix}6-1\\\\-4-4\\end{pmatrix}=\\begin{pmatrix}5\\\\-8\\end{pmatrix}$.","w":["Added $\\mathbf{b}$ instead of subtracting it.","","Worked out $\\mathbf{a}-2\\mathbf{b}$, doubling the wrong vector.","Sign slip in the bottom: $-4-4$ is $-8$, not $0$."]}
{"id":"gma-044","s":"maths","t":"P1-P5","lv":1,"q":"The probability that a counter drawn from a bag is blue is 0.35. What is the probability that it is not blue?","o":["$0.35$","$0.45$","$0.7$","$0.65$"],"a":3,"x":"Probabilities of an event and its opposite add to 1. $1-0.35=0.65$.","w":["Gave the probability of blue, not of 'not blue'.","Subtraction slip: $1-0.35$ is $0.65$, not $0.45$.","Doubled $0.35$ rather than subtracting it from 1.",""]}
{"id":"gma-045","s":"maths","t":"P6-P8","lv":3,"q":"Bag $A$ gives a red counter with probability 0.3. Bag $B$ gives red with probability 0.4. One counter is taken from each bag. What is P(at least one red)?","o":["$0.12$","$0.46$","$0.58$","$0.7$"],"a":2,"x":"P(no red) $=0.7\\times0.6=0.42$. So P(at least one red) $=1-0.42=0.58$.","w":["Multiplied $0.3\\times0.4$, which is P(both red).","That is P(exactly one red); it leaves out both red.","","Added $0.3+0.4$; both can be red, so that case is counted twice."]}
{"id":"gma-046","s":"maths","t":"P9","lv":3,"q":"Of 20 boys, 14 play football. Of 20 girls, 9 play football. A football player is chosen at random. What is the probability they are a girl?","o":["$\\frac{9}{20}$","$\\frac{9}{40}$","$\\frac{9}{23}$","$\\frac{11}{23}$"],"a":2,"x":"Football players: $14+9=23$. Of these, 9 are girls, so the probability is $\\frac{9}{23}$.","w":["Divided by 20 girls instead of all 23 football players.","Divided by all 40 pupils; the group is only football players.","","Used the 11 girls who don't play; should be the 9 who do."]}
{"id":"gma-047","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":2,"q":"A school has 600 pupils, 180 of them in Year 10. A sample of 50 pupils is taken, with each year group in the same proportion as in the school. How many should be from Year 10?","o":["$15$","$18$","$30$","$10$"],"a":0,"x":"Year 10 is $\\frac{180}{600}=30\\%$ of the school. $30\\%$ of 50 is 15.","w":["","Divided 180 by 10; you need 30% of the sample of 50, which is 15.","Gave the percentage 30 itself, not 30% of 50.","10 is 20% of 50, not 30%; the Year 10 share is $\\frac{180}{600}$."]}
{"id":"gma-048","s":"maths","t":"S3","lv":3,"q":"A histogram has a bar for $0 \\le t < 10$ of height 3 and a bar for $10 \\le t < 30$ of height 1.5. The vertical axis is frequency density. What is the total frequency of the two bars?","o":["$4.5$","$30$","$90$","$60$"],"a":3,"x":"Frequency $=$ frequency density $\\times$ class width. $3\\times10=30$ and $1.5\\times20=30$. Total $60$.","w":["Added the heights; frequency is density $\\times$ class width.","Only found the first bar's frequency; the second bar adds another 30.","Multiplied 3 by 30; the class widths are 10 and 20.",""]}
{"id":"gma-049","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":2,"q":"The mean of five numbers is 8. A sixth number is added and the mean becomes 9. What is the sixth number?","o":["$9$","$14$","$10$","$17$"],"a":1,"x":"Total of five $=40$. Total of six $=54$. The new number is $54-40=14$.","w":["Took the new mean as the new number; the total must rise from $40$ to $54$.","","Added $1$ to the new mean; it must give $1$ extra to each of 5 others: $9+5=14$.","Added the two means, $8+9$; the new number is the difference in totals, $54-40$."]}
{"id":"gma-050","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":1,"q":"As the temperature rises, sales of hot drinks fall. What type of correlation is this?","o":["Positive correlation","Negative correlation","No correlation","Perfect correlation"],"a":1,"x":"One quantity increases while the other decreases, so the correlation is negative.","w":["Positive means both rise together; here sales fall as temperature rises.","","There is a clear link between temperature and sales, so correlation does exist.","Perfect describes points exactly on a line (strength), not the direction of the trend."]}
{"id":"gma-051","s":"maths","t":"N1-N3","lv":1,"q":"Write these decimals in order, smallest first: $0.45$, $0.5$, $0.405$, $0.054$","o":["$0.5,\\ 0.45,\\ 0.054,\\ 0.405$","$0.054,\\ 0.405,\\ 0.45,\\ 0.5$","$0.5,\\ 0.45,\\ 0.405,\\ 0.054$","$0.054,\\ 0.45,\\ 0.405,\\ 0.5$"],"a":1,"x":"Write each to 3 decimal places: $0.054$, $0.405$, $0.450$, $0.500$. Compare place by place. Longer decimals are not bigger, and the order is smallest first.","w":["Read the digits after the point as whole numbers ($5, 45, 54, 405$), so longer decimals looked bigger.","","This is largest first. The question asks for smallest first.","Put $0.45$ before $0.405$. Written as $0.450$ and $0.405$, $0.405$ is smaller."]}
{"id":"gma-052","s":"maths","t":"N1-N3","lv":1,"q":"Work out $\\frac{3}{4} \\div \\frac{2}{5}$.","o":["$\\frac{3}{10}$","$\\frac{8}{15}$","$\\frac{7}{8}$","$1\\frac{7}{8}$"],"a":3,"x":"Keep the first fraction, flip the second and multiply: $\\frac{3}{4}\\times\\frac{5}{2}=\\frac{15}{8}=1\\frac{7}{8}$. Do not flip the first fraction, and keep the whole number.","w":["Multiplied the two fractions instead of dividing. To divide, flip the second fraction and multiply.","Flipped the first fraction, $\\frac{4}{3}\\times\\frac{2}{5}$. Only the second fraction is flipped.","Wrote only the remainder of $\\frac{15}{8}$ and lost the whole number $1$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-053","s":"maths","t":"N1-N3","lv":2,"q":"Work out $(6.4 - 2.8) \\div 0.4 + 3 \\times 2$.","o":["$24$","$6.9$","$15$","$5.4$"],"a":2,"x":"Brackets first: $3.6$. Then $3.6\\div0.4=9$ and $3\\times2=6$. Add: $9+6=15$. The traps are working left to right and misplacing the decimal point.","w":["Added $9+3$ before multiplying by $2$. Multiplication must be done before addition.","Got $3.6\\div0.4=0.9$ instead of $9$. Dividing by $0.4$ makes the number bigger.","","Ignored the brackets and did $2.8\\div0.4$ first. Brackets come first."]}
{"id":"gma-054","s":"maths","t":"N1-N3","lv":3,"q":"Mia reads $\\frac{2}{5}$ of a book on Monday. On Tuesday she reads $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the part that is left. What fraction of the whole book is still unread?","o":["$\\frac{9}{20}$","$\\frac{7}{20}$","$\\frac{13}{20}$","$\\frac{3}{5}$"],"a":0,"x":"After Monday, $\\frac{3}{5}$ is left. Tuesday she reads $\\frac{1}{4}\\times\\frac{3}{5}=\\frac{3}{20}$. Unread: $\\frac{3}{5}-\\frac{3}{20}=\\frac{12}{20}-\\frac{3}{20}=\\frac{9}{20}$. The trap is taking $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the whole book.","w":["","Took $\\frac{1}{4}$ of the whole book, not of what was left: $1-\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{1}{4}$.","Added $\\frac{2}{5}+\\frac{1}{4}$ to get the part read, not the part unread, and used the wrong whole.","Only subtracted Monday's reading and forgot Tuesday's."]}
{"id":"gma-055","s":"maths","t":"N4-N5","lv":1,"q":"Which of these numbers is a prime number?","o":["$51$","$57$","$53$","$91$"],"a":2,"x":"53 has no factors except 1 and 53, so it is prime. The traps are 51 (3 × 17), 57 (3 × 19) and 91 (7 × 13), which look prime but are not.","w":["51 = 3 × 17 because its digits add to 6, so it is a multiple of 3 and not prime.","57 = 3 × 19 because its digits add to 12, so it is a multiple of 3 and not prime.","","91 = 7 × 13, so it is not prime, even though it is not in the usual times tables."]}
{"id":"gma-057","s":"maths","t":"N4-N5","lv":2,"q":"Bus A leaves a station every 12 minutes. Bus B leaves every 18 minutes. Both leave together at 8:00 am. When do they next leave together?","o":["8:06 am","8:30 am","11:36 am","8:36 am"],"a":3,"x":"We need the lowest common multiple of 12 and 18, which is 36. So the next time is 36 minutes after 8:00, at 8:36 am. The traps are using the HCF (6) or multiplying (216).","w":["This uses 6, the HCF of 12 and 18, but a 'together again' question needs the LCM.","30 is not a multiple of 12 or 18, so neither bus leaves at 8:30 am.","This uses 12 × 18 = 216 minutes, which is a common multiple but not the lowest one.",""]}
{"id":"gma-058","s":"maths","t":"N4-N5","lv":2,"q":"$A = 2^3 \\times 3^2 \\times 5$ and $B = 2^2 \\times 3^4 \\times 7$. What is the highest common factor (HCF) of $A$ and $B$?","o":["$2^2 \\times 3^2$","$2^3 \\times 3^4$","$2 \\times 3$","$2^3 \\times 3^4 \\times 5 \\times 7$"],"a":0,"x":"For the HCF, take only the primes in both numbers, each to its lowest power. That gives $2^2 \\times 3^2$. The trap is taking the highest powers, which is the LCM method.","w":["","This takes the highest powers of the common primes, but the HCF needs the lowest powers.","This ignores the powers, but both numbers share $2^2$ and $3^2$, so the HCF is bigger.","This is the LCM, using every prime to its highest power, not the HCF."]}
{"id":"gma-059","s":"maths","t":"N4-N5","lv":3,"q":"A 3-digit code uses the digits 0 to 9. No digit can be used twice. The first digit cannot be 0. How many different codes are possible?","o":["$720$","$729$","$648$","$900$"],"a":2,"x":"First digit: 9 choices (not 0). Second digit: 9 choices (0 is now allowed, but not the first digit). Third digit: 8 choices. $9 \\times 9 \\times 8 = 648$.","w":["10 × 9 × 8 ignores the rule that the first digit cannot be 0.","9 × 9 × 9 wrongly gives the third digit 9 choices, but two digits are already used, leaving 8.","","9 × 10 × 10 allows repeated digits, but the code cannot use a digit twice."]}
{"id":"gma-060","s":"maths","t":"N6-N7","lv":1,"q":"Write $7^{-2}$ as a fraction.","o":["$\\dfrac{1}{49}$","$-49$","$-14$","$\\dfrac{1}{14}$"],"a":0,"x":"A negative power means a reciprocal: $7^{-2}=\\dfrac{1}{7^2}=\\dfrac{1}{49}$. The trap is thinking a negative power makes the answer negative.","w":["","Treated the negative power as a negative answer; it means reciprocal, not negative.","Did $7\\times(-2)$; the power is not multiplied by the base.","Found the reciprocal but did $7\\times2=14$ instead of squaring 7."]}
{"id":"gma-061","s":"maths","t":"N6-N7","lv":1,"q":"Work out $\\sqrt{49}+\\sqrt[3]{27}$.","o":["$16$","$10$","$27.5$","$34$"],"a":1,"x":"$\\sqrt{49}=7$ and $\\sqrt[3]{27}=3$ because $3\\times3\\times3=27$. So the total is $10$. The trap is dividing by the root number instead of finding the root.","w":["Used $27\\div3=9$ for the cube root; a cube root is the number multiplied by itself three times to give 27.","","Used $49\\div2=24.5$ for the square root; a square root is not halving.","Found $\\sqrt{49}=7$ but left the 27 unchanged instead of finding its cube root."]}
{"id":"gma-062","s":"maths","t":"N6-N7","lv":2,"q":"Simplify $\\dfrac{6x^5y^2\\times 2x^3y}{4x^2y^3}$.","o":["$3x^4$","$8x^6$","$3x^6$","$3x^6y^6$"],"a":2,"x":"Top: $12x^8y^3$. Divide by $4x^2y^3$: $12\\div4=3$, $x^{8-2}=x^6$, $y^{3-3}=y^0=1$. So $3x^6$. The trap is mishandling the powers of $y$ or the coefficients.","w":["Did $8\\div2=4$ for the powers of $x$; you subtract powers when dividing.","Subtracted coefficients ($12-4$); you divide the numbers when dividing terms.","","Added all the powers of $y$; the $y^3$ on the bottom should be subtracted, leaving $y^0=1$."]}
{"id":"gma-063","s":"maths","t":"N6-N7","lv":2,"q":"Work out the value of $8^{\\frac{2}{3}}$.","o":["$64$","$\\dfrac{16}{3}$","$2$","$4$"],"a":3,"x":"The denominator 3 means cube root: $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2$. The numerator 2 means square: $2^2=4$. The trap is using only one part of the fraction.","w":["Squared 8 but ignored the cube root in the denominator.","Multiplied $8\\times\\frac{2}{3}$; a fractional power is not multiplication.","Took the cube root but forgot to square it for the numerator 2.",""]}
{"id":"gma-064","s":"maths","t":"N6-N7","lv":3,"q":"Given that $\\dfrac{3^n\\times 9^2}{27}=3^7$, work out the value of $n$.","o":["$8$","$6$","$0$","$3$"],"a":1,"x":"Write all in base 3: $9^2=3^4$ and $27=3^3$. Then $3^{n+4-3}=3^{n+1}=3^7$, so $n=6$. The trap is not changing $9^2$ into a power of 3.","w":["Treated $9^2$ as $3^2$, giving $3^{n-1}=3^7$; $9^2=3^4$.","","Added the 3 instead of subtracting for the division, giving $n+7=7$.","Forgot to divide by 27, giving $n+4=7$."]}
{"id":"gma-065","s":"maths","t":"N8","lv":1,"q":"Simplify $\\sqrt{50}$.","o":["$25\\sqrt{2}$","$5\\sqrt{2}$","$2\\sqrt{5}$","$5\\sqrt{10}$"],"a":1,"x":"$\\sqrt{50}=\\sqrt{25\\times2}=\\sqrt{25}\\times\\sqrt{2}=5\\sqrt{2}$. The trap is forgetting to square root the 25 when you take it out.","w":["Took 25 out of the root without square rooting it; it must become 5.","","Swapped the numbers. $2\\sqrt{5}$ squares to 20, not 50.","Took 5 out but left 10 inside the root. $5\\sqrt{10}$ squares to 250."]}
{"id":"gma-066","s":"maths","t":"N8","lv":1,"q":"A semicircle has a diameter of 8 cm. What is its area, in terms of $\\pi$?","o":["$16\\pi$ cm²","$32\\pi$ cm²","$4\\pi$ cm²","$8\\pi$ cm²"],"a":3,"x":"Radius $=4$. Full circle $=\\pi\\times4^2=16\\pi$. A semicircle is half, so $8\\pi$. Traps: using the diameter as the radius, or forgetting to halve.","w":["This is the full circle. You forgot to halve it for a semicircle.","Used the diameter 8 as the radius, then halved. You must halve the diameter first.","Found half the circumference, $\\pi d\\div2$, which is the curved edge length, not the area.",""]}
{"id":"gma-067","s":"maths","t":"N8","lv":2,"q":"Expand and simplify $(3+\\sqrt{2})(3-\\sqrt{2})$.","o":["$7$","$11$","$9$","$5$"],"a":0,"x":"$9-3\\sqrt{2}+3\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{2}\\times\\sqrt{2}=9-2=7$. The middle terms cancel, and $\\sqrt{2}\\times\\sqrt{2}=2$.","w":["","Added 2 instead of subtracting. The last term is $-\\sqrt{2}\\times\\sqrt{2}=-2$.","Ignored the last term. $-\\sqrt{2}\\times\\sqrt{2}$ gives $-2$, which must be subtracted.","Treated $\\sqrt{2}\\times\\sqrt{2}$ as 4. It equals 2."]}
{"id":"gma-068","s":"maths","t":"N8","lv":2,"q":"Rationalise the denominator and simplify $\\dfrac{12}{\\sqrt{6}}$.","o":["$12\\sqrt{6}$","$\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$","$2\\sqrt{6}$","$6\\sqrt{6}$"],"a":2,"x":"Multiply top and bottom by $\\sqrt{6}$: $\\dfrac{12\\sqrt{6}}{6}=2\\sqrt{6}$. The trap is forgetting to divide by the new denominator 6.","w":["Multiplied the top by $\\sqrt{6}$ but forgot to divide by the new denominator 6.","Divided the wrong way round: $\\dfrac{12\\sqrt{6}}{6}$ is $2\\sqrt{6}$, not $\\dfrac{\\sqrt{6}}{2}$.","","Divided by 2 instead of 6. The new denominator is $\\sqrt{6}\\times\\sqrt{6}=6$."]}
{"id":"gma-069","s":"maths","t":"N8","lv":3,"q":"A rectangle has area 24 cm² and length $4\\sqrt{3}$ cm. Work out its width as a simplified exact value with no surd in a denominator.","o":["$6\\sqrt{3}$ cm","$2\\sqrt{3}$ cm","$3\\sqrt{2}$ cm","$2\\sqrt{6}$ cm"],"a":1,"x":"Width $=\\dfrac{24}{4\\sqrt{3}}=\\dfrac{6}{\\sqrt{3}}=\\dfrac{6\\sqrt{3}}{3}=2\\sqrt{3}$. The trap is forgetting to divide by 3 after multiplying by $\\sqrt{3}$.","w":["Multiplied by $\\sqrt{3}$ but forgot to divide by the new denominator 3.","","Mixed up the numbers inside and outside the root. $3\\sqrt{2}$ squares to 18, but the width squared is 12.","Wrong surd: $2\\sqrt{6}$ squares to 24, but the width squared is 12."]}
{"id":"gma-070","s":"maths","t":"N9","lv":1,"q":"Write $3.6\\times10^{5}$ as an ordinary number.","o":["$36\\,000$","$3\\,600\\,000$","$360\\,000$","$0.000036$"],"a":2,"x":"A power of 5 means move the decimal point 5 places to the right: $3.6\\to360\\,000$. Count the places carefully and don't just add zeros.","w":["Moved the point only 4 places; the power 5 needs 5 places.","Moved the point 6 places, probably by writing 5 zeros after the 36.","","Treated the power as negative; a positive power gives a large number."]}
{"id":"gma-071","s":"maths","t":"N9","lv":1,"q":"Which of these is written in standard form?","o":["$7.2\\times10^{-5}$","$0.6\\times10^{4}$","$12\\times10^{-3}$","$45\\times10^{2}$"],"a":0,"x":"Standard form is $A\\times10^n$ with $1\\le A<10$ and $n$ a whole number. Only $7.2\\times10^{-5}$ fits. The others have $A$ below 1 or above 10.","w":["","$0.6$ is less than 1, so it is not standard form.","$12$ is more than 10, so it is not standard form.","$45$ is more than 10, so it is not standard form."]}
{"id":"gma-072","s":"maths","t":"N9","lv":2,"q":"Work out $(4\\times10^{3})\\times(6\\times10^{5})$. Give your answer in standard form.","o":["$2.4\\times10^{8}$","$24\\times10^{8}$","$2.4\\times10^{15}$","$2.4\\times10^{9}$"],"a":3,"x":"$4\\times6=24$ and $10^3\\times10^5=10^8$, so $24\\times10^8$. Then $24=2.4\\times10$, giving $2.4\\times10^9$. The trap is not adjusting the power.","w":["Changed 24 to 2.4 but forgot to add 1 to the power.","$24$ is not between 1 and 10, so this is not standard form.","Multiplied the powers ($3\\times5$) instead of adding them.",""]}
{"id":"gma-073","s":"maths","t":"N9","lv":2,"q":"Work out $3.2\\times10^{4}+5\\times10^{3}$. Give your answer in standard form.","o":["$8.2\\times10^{4}$","$3.7\\times10^{4}$","$3.7\\times10^{3}$","$8.2\\times10^{7}$"],"a":1,"x":"Write both as ordinary numbers: $32\\,000+5\\,000=37\\,000=3.7\\times10^4$. You can't just add the front numbers when the powers differ.","w":["Added $3.2+5$ and ignored the different powers.","","Right digits but the power is wrong; $37\\,000$ needs $10^4$.","Added the front numbers and also added the powers."]}
{"id":"gma-074","s":"maths","t":"N9","lv":3,"q":"One dust particle has mass $1.6\\times10^{-9}$ g. How many of these particles are in $8$ g? Give your answer in standard form.","o":["$5\\times10^{-9}$","$1.28\\times10^{-8}$","$5\\times10^{9}$","$5\\times10^{8}$"],"a":2,"x":"Divide the total mass by one mass: $8\\div(1.6\\times10^{-9})=5\\times10^{9}$. Subtracting powers, $0-(-9)=+9$. The count must be huge, so the power is positive.","w":["Divided but kept the negative power; the answer must be a large number.","Multiplied the masses instead of dividing.","","Slipped when subtracting powers; $0-(-9)$ is $9$, not $8$."]}
{"id":"gma-075","s":"maths","t":"N10-N12","lv":1,"q":"Write $\\frac{7}{25}$ as a decimal.","o":["$0.725$","$3.57$","$0.28$","$0.028$"],"a":2,"x":"Multiply top and bottom by 4 to get $\\frac{28}{100}=0.28$. Or work out $7\\div25$. The trap is joining the digits or dividing the wrong way round.","w":["Joined the digits 7 and 25 after the decimal point; a fraction is a division, not two digits.","Worked out $25\\div7$ instead of $7\\div25$, dividing the wrong way round.","","Put the digits in the wrong place; $\\frac{28}{100}$ is $0.28$, not thousandths."]}
{"id":"gma-076","s":"maths","t":"N10-N12","lv":1,"q":"Work out $35\\%$ of £240.","o":["£72","£96","£84","£8.40"],"a":2,"x":"$10\\%$ is £24, so $30\\%$ is £72. $5\\%$ is half of $10\\%$, which is £12. Total is £84. The trap is adding £24 instead of £12 for the $5\\%$.","w":["Only found $30\\%$ and forgot to add the extra $5\\%$.","Added £24 (a full $10\\%$) for the last $5\\%$, instead of half of it, £12.","","Placed the decimal point wrongly; this is $3.5\\%$ of £240, not $35\\%$."]}
{"id":"gma-077","s":"maths","t":"N10-N12","lv":2,"q":"$\\frac{3}{5}$ of a number is $72$. What is the number?","o":["$43.2$","$24$","$360$","$120$"],"a":3,"x":"$\\frac{3}{5}$ is $72$, so $\\frac{1}{5}$ is $72\\div3=24$. The whole is $5\\times24=120$. The trap is finding $\\frac{3}{5}$ of $72$ instead.","w":["Found $\\frac{3}{5}$ of $72$; that works forwards, but here $72$ is already the $\\frac{3}{5}$.","Stopped after dividing by 3; $24$ is only one fifth of the number.","Multiplied $72$ by 5 without first dividing by 3 to find one fifth.",""]}
{"id":"gma-078","s":"maths","t":"N10-N12","lv":2,"q":"Which of these is the largest?","o":["$\\frac{3}{8}$","$\\frac{7}{20}$","$0.36$","$33\\%$"],"a":0,"x":"Change all to decimals: $0.375$, $0.35$, $0.36$, $0.33$. The largest is $0.375$, which is $\\frac{3}{8}$. The trap is not converting to the same form.","w":["","$\\frac{7}{20}=0.35$, which is smaller than $0.375$.","Chose the decimal that looks biggest, but $\\frac{3}{8}=0.375$ is larger than $0.36$.","$33\\%=0.33$, which is actually the smallest of the four values."]}
{"id":"gma-079","s":"maths","t":"N10-N12","lv":3,"q":"Write the recurring decimal $0.1\\dot{6}$ (that is, $0.16666\\ldots$) as a fraction in its simplest form.","o":["$\\frac{16}{99}$","$\\frac{4}{25}$","$\\frac{1}{6}$","$\\frac{8}{45}$"],"a":2,"x":"Let $x=0.1666\\ldots$. Then $100x-10x=16.666\\ldots-1.666\\ldots$, so $90x=15$ and $x=\\frac{1}{6}$. The trap is subtracting wrongly or treating both digits as recurring.","w":["Treated both digits as recurring ($0.\\dot{1}\\dot{6}$); here only the 6 repeats.","Treated it as the terminating decimal $0.16$, ignoring the recurring 6.","","Used $100x-10x=16$ instead of $15$; the subtraction is $16.666\\ldots-1.666\\ldots=15$."]}
{"id":"gma-081","s":"maths","t":"N13-N16","lv":1,"q":"Estimate the value of $\\dfrac{19.6\\times4.1}{0.52}$ by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","o":["$160$","$16$","$40$","$1600$"],"a":0,"x":"Round to $\\dfrac{20\\times4}{0.5}$. The top is $80$. Dividing by $0.5$ doubles it, giving $160$. The trap is mishandling the division by $0.5$.","w":["","Treated dividing by $0.5$ as dividing by $5$, which makes the answer ten times too small.","Multiplied by $0.5$ instead of dividing by it.","Treated $0.52$ as $0.05$, which makes the answer ten times too big."]}
{"id":"gma-082","s":"maths","t":"N13-N16","lv":2,"q":"A number $n$ is truncated to 1 decimal place and the result is $3.8$. Which is the error interval for $n$?","o":["$3.75 \\le n < 3.85$","$3.8 \\le n < 3.9$","$3.8 \\le n \\le 3.9$","$3.75 < n \\le 3.85$"],"a":1,"x":"Truncating just cuts off digits with no rounding. So $n$ can be $3.8$ up to but not including $3.9$, because $3.9$ truncates to $3.9$. The trap is using rounding rules.","w":["This is the interval for rounding to 1 d.p., not truncating, which cuts digits off.","","Includes $3.9$, but $3.9$ truncates to $3.9$, not $3.8$, so the top needs $<$.","Mixed up the inequality signs and used the rounding interval; truncation starts exactly at $3.8$."]}
{"id":"gma-083","s":"maths","t":"N13-N16","lv":2,"q":"A rectangle has length $8$ cm and width $5$ cm. Each is measured to the nearest cm. What is the upper bound of the area?","o":["$40$ cm²","$54$ cm²","$42.5$ cm²","$46.75$ cm²"],"a":3,"x":"Upper bounds are $8.5$ cm and $5.5$ cm. Area $=8.5\\times5.5=46.75$ cm². The trap is adding a whole unit, or only changing one measurement.","w":["Multiplied the measured values, $8\\times5$, with no bounds used.","Used $9$ and $6$, adding a whole unit instead of half a unit.","Used $8.5\\times5$, raising only one of the two measurements to its upper bound.",""]}
{"id":"gma-084","s":"maths","t":"N13-N16","lv":3,"q":"A car travels $120$ m, measured to the nearest $10$ m, in $15$ s, measured to the nearest second. Find the lower bound of its speed in m/s, to 3 significant figures.","o":["$7.42$ m/s","$7.93$ m/s","$8.00$ m/s","$8.62$ m/s"],"a":0,"x":"Speed $=$ distance $\\div$ time. For the smallest speed use the smallest distance, $115$, and the largest time, $15.5$. $115\\div15.5=7.42$. The trap is using the lower bound for both.","w":["","Used the lower bound for both distance and time, $115\\div14.5$, which is not the smallest speed.","Used $120\\div15$, so no bounds were used at all.","Used the upper distance and lower time, $125\\div14.5$, which gives the upper bound of speed."]}
{"id":"gma-085","s":"maths","t":"A1-A3","lv":1,"q":"Which one of these is an identity?","o":["$3x+2=11$","$2(x+3)\\equiv 2x+6$","$x^2=16$","$x+5>2$"],"a":1,"x":"An identity is true for every value of $x$. Expanding $2(x+3)$ always gives $2x+6$. The others are true only for some values, so they are equations or an inequality.","w":["This is an equation. It is true only when $x=3$, not for every value.","","This is an equation. It is true only for $x=4$ and $x=-4$, so it is not an identity.","This is an inequality. It is true for many values of $x$ but not all, so it is not an identity."]}
{"id":"gma-086","s":"maths","t":"A1-A3","lv":1,"q":"Which one of these is an expression?","o":["$4n-7$","$4n-7=9$","$4n-7>9$","$4n-7\\equiv 4n-7$"],"a":0,"x":"An expression has no equals sign and no inequality sign. It is a group of terms like $4n-7$. Adding $=$, $>$ or $\\equiv$ makes an equation, inequality or identity.","w":["","This is an equation because it has an equals sign. People wrongly call anything with letters an expression.","This is an inequality because of the $>$ sign, not an expression.","This is an identity because it uses $\\equiv$ and is true for all $n$. It is not an expression."]}
{"id":"gma-087","s":"maths","t":"A1-A3","lv":2,"q":"Simplify $a\\times 4\\times a\\times b$.","o":["$4ab$","$8ab$","$16a^2b^2$","$4a^2b$"],"a":3,"x":"Write the number first, then letters. $a\\times a=a^2$, so the answer is $4a^2b$. The trap is writing $a\\times a$ as $a$ or $2a$.","w":["Lost a power: $a\\times a$ is $a^2$, not $a$.","Added the letters instead of multiplying: $a\\times a$ is not $2a$.","Squared everything, including the 4 and $b$. Only $a$ appears twice.",""]}
{"id":"gma-088","s":"maths","t":"A1-A3","lv":2,"q":"Use $v^2=u^2+2as$ to find $v$ (positive) when $u=5$, $a=2$ and $s=6$.","o":["$v=49$","$v=\\sqrt{29}$","$v=7$","$v=\\sqrt{37}$"],"a":2,"x":"$v^2=5^2+2\\times2\\times6=25+24=49$. Then $v=\\sqrt{49}=7$. The trap is stopping at $v^2$ or not squaring $u$.","w":["This is $v^2$. You must take the square root to find $v$.","Used $u$ instead of $u^2$: $5+24=29$. The 5 must be squared first.","","Left out the 2 in $2as$, using $as=12$. The formula has $2as=24$."]}
{"id":"gma-089","s":"maths","t":"A1-A3","lv":3,"q":"$T=\\dfrac{3p-q^2}{2}$. Work out $T$ when $p=-2$ and $q=-4$.","o":["$-11$","$5$","$-14$","$-22$"],"a":0,"x":"$3p=-6$ and $q^2=(-4)^2=16$. So the top is $-6-16=-22$. Divide it all by 2 to get $-11$. Traps are $q^2=-16$ and dividing only part of the top.","w":["","Used $q^2=-16$, giving $(-6+16)/2$. A negative number squared is positive.","Divided only $q^2$ by 2: $-6-8=-14$. The whole top is divided by 2.","Forgot to divide by 2 at the end, leaving the top as $-22$."]}
{"id":"gma-090","s":"maths","t":"A4","lv":1,"q":"Write $\\sqrt{48}$ in the form $a\\sqrt{3}$, where $a$ is an integer.","o":["$16\\sqrt{3}$","$4\\sqrt{3}$","$8\\sqrt{6}$","$3\\sqrt{16}$"],"a":1,"x":"$48=16\\times3$, so $\\sqrt{48}=\\sqrt{16}\\times\\sqrt{3}=4\\sqrt{3}$. The trap is forgetting to square root the square number 16.","w":["Left the 16 as it is. The square number must be square rooted to give 4.","","Wrong split: $8\\sqrt{6}$ is about 19.6, but $\\sqrt{48}$ is about 6.9.","Swapped the factors, which is not a simplification. $3\\sqrt{16}=12$, not $\\sqrt{48}$."]}
{"id":"gma-091","s":"maths","t":"A4","lv":1,"q":"Factorise fully $6x^2y-9xy^2$.","o":["$3x(2xy-3y^2)$","$3xy(2x+3y)$","$3xy(2x-3y)$","$3xy(2x-3)$"],"a":2,"x":"The highest common factor is $3xy$. Dividing gives $2x$ and $3y$, so $3xy(2x-3y)$. The trap is taking out only part of the common factor.","w":["Correct when expanded, but not fully factorised. A common factor $y$ is still inside the bracket.","Sign error: the second term is $-9xy^2$, so the bracket needs a minus sign.","","Lost the $y$ in the second term. $3xy\\times(-3)=-9xy$, not $-9xy^2$."]}
{"id":"gma-092","s":"maths","t":"A4","lv":2,"q":"Expand and simplify $(3+\\sqrt{5})(2-\\sqrt{5})$.","o":["$6-\\sqrt{5}-\\sqrt{10}$","$11-\\sqrt{5}$","$1+\\sqrt{5}$","$1-\\sqrt{5}$"],"a":3,"x":"$6-3\\sqrt{5}+2\\sqrt{5}-5=1-\\sqrt{5}$. The trap is $\\sqrt{5}\\times\\sqrt{5}=5$, so $\\sqrt{5}\\times(-\\sqrt{5})$ gives $-5$.","w":["Wrote $\\sqrt{5}\\times\\sqrt{5}$ as $\\sqrt{10}$. The product is 5, which combines with the 6.","Treated the last term as $+5$ instead of $-5$, so the sign is wrong.","Combined $-3\\sqrt{5}+2\\sqrt{5}$ as $+\\sqrt{5}$. It equals $-\\sqrt{5}$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-093","s":"maths","t":"A4","lv":3,"q":"Expand and simplify $(2x-1)(x+4)(x-3)$.","o":["$2x^3+x^2-25x+12$","$2x^3+x^2-25x-12$","$2x^3+13x^2+17x-12$","$2x^3+x^2-21x+12$"],"a":0,"x":"$(2x-1)(x+4)=2x^2+7x-4$. Then multiply by $(x-3)$: $2x^3-6x^2+7x^2-21x-4x+12=2x^3+x^2-25x+12$. Watch the signs.","w":["","Sign slip on the constant: $(-4)\\times(-3)=+12$, not $-12$.","Used $x+3$ instead of $x-3$ in the last bracket, so several signs are wrong.","Missed the $-4x$ term from $-4\\times x$, so the $x$ terms were not combined correctly."]}
{"id":"gma-094","s":"maths","t":"A5","lv":1,"q":"Use $v=u+at$ with $u=4$, $a=3$ and $t=5$. Work out the value of $v$.","o":["$35$","$12$","$19$","$60$"],"a":2,"x":"Follow BIDMAS. Multiply first: $at=3\\times5=15$. Then add $u$: $v=4+15=19$. The trap is adding before multiplying.","w":["Added $u$ and $a$ first, then multiplied by $t$. Multiplication must be done before addition.","Added all three numbers. The formula has $at$, which means $a\\times t$.","","Multiplied all three numbers. The formula adds $u$ to $at$."]}
{"id":"gma-095","s":"maths","t":"A5","lv":1,"q":"Make $m$ the subject of $y=mx+c$.","o":["$m=\\frac{y-c}{x}$","$m=y-\\frac{c}{x}$","$m=\\frac{y+c}{x}$","$m=\\frac{y}{x}-c$"],"a":0,"x":"Subtract $c$ from both sides: $y-c=mx$. Then divide the whole of the left side by $x$: $m=\\frac{y-c}{x}$. The trap is dividing only one term.","w":["","Divided only $c$ by $x$. Every term on the left must be divided by $x$.","Sign slip: $c$ is added on the right, so subtract it from both sides.","Divided only $y$ by $x$. The $c$ must be divided by $x$ too."]}
{"id":"gma-096","s":"maths","t":"A5","lv":2,"q":"Make $h$ the subject of $V=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi r^2h$.","o":["$h=\\frac{V}{3\\pi r^2}$","$h=3V\\pi r^2$","$h=\\frac{3V}{\\pi r}$","$h=\\frac{3V}{\\pi r^2}$"],"a":3,"x":"Multiply both sides by 3: $3V=\\pi r^2h$. Then divide by $\\pi r^2$: $h=\\frac{3V}{\\pi r^2}$. The trap is dividing by 3 instead of multiplying.","w":["Divided $V$ by 3. Undo $\\frac{1}{3}$ by multiplying by 3.","Multiplied by $\\pi r^2$. It is multiplied with $h$, so divide by it.","Divided by $\\pi r$ not $\\pi r^2$. The $r$ is squared, so keep the square.",""]}
{"id":"gma-098","s":"maths","t":"A5","lv":3,"q":"Make $l$ the subject of $T=2\\sqrt{\\frac{l}{g}}$.","o":["$l=\\frac{gT^2}{2}$","$l=4gT^2$","$l=\\frac{gT^2}{4}$","$l=\\frac{gT}{4}$"],"a":2,"x":"Divide by 2: $\\frac{T}{2}=\\sqrt{\\frac{l}{g}}$. Square both sides: $\\frac{T^2}{4}=\\frac{l}{g}$. Multiply by $g$: $l=\\frac{gT^2}{4}$. The trap is not squaring the 2.","w":["Squared $T$ but not the 2. The whole of $\\frac{T}{2}$ must be squared.","Multiplied by 4 instead of dividing. Squaring $\\frac{T}{2}$ gives $\\frac{T^2}{4}$.","","Squared the 2 but not $T$. Squaring both sides gives $T^2$, not $T$."]}
{"id":"gma-099","s":"maths","t":"A6-A7","lv":1,"q":"$n$ is any integer. Which expression always gives an odd number?","o":["$n+1$","$2n+1$","$3n$","$n^2+1$"],"a":1,"x":"$2n$ is always even, so $2n+1$ is always odd. The trap is to pick an expression that is odd only for some values of $n$. Testing one value of $n$ is not a proof.","w":["When $n$ is odd, $n+1$ is even, so it is not always odd.","","When $n$ is even, $3n$ is even, so it is not always odd.","When $n$ is odd, $n^2$ is odd and $n^2+1$ is even."]}
{"id":"gma-100","s":"maths","t":"A6-A7","lv":2,"q":"$f(x)=2x+1$ and $g(x)=x^2$. Which expression is $fg(x)$?","o":["$(2x+1)^2$","$4x^2+1$","$2x^2+1$","$2x^3+x^2$"],"a":2,"x":"$fg(x)$ means do $g$ first, then $f$. So $fg(x)=f(x^2)=2x^2+1$. The trap is the order: doing $f$ first gives $gf(x)=(2x+1)^2$.","w":["This is $gf(x)$: $f$ was done first. For $fg(x)$, $g$ goes first.","Squared the 2 as well as the $x$, giving $(2x)^2$ instead of $2x^2$.","","Multiplied $f(x)$ by $g(x)$ instead of putting $g(x)$ into $f$."]}
{"id":"gma-101","s":"maths","t":"A6-A7","lv":2,"q":"$f(x)=\\dfrac{2x+5}{3}$. Find $f^{-1}(x)$.","o":["$\\dfrac{3x-5}{2}$","$\\dfrac{3x+5}{2}$","$\\dfrac{2x-5}{3}$","$\\dfrac{3x}{2}-5$"],"a":0,"x":"Let $y=\\frac{2x+5}{3}$. Then $3y=2x+5$, so $x=\\frac{3y-5}{2}$. Swap letters to get $f^{-1}(x)=\\frac{3x-5}{2}$. The trap is undoing the steps in the wrong order.","w":["","Multiplied by 3 correctly but added 5 instead of subtracting it.","Only changed the sign of 5 and kept $\\frac{2x}{3}$, so the operations were not inverted.","Subtracted 5 after multiplying by $\\frac32$, which means undoing the steps in the wrong order."]}
{"id":"gma-102","s":"maths","t":"A6-A7","lv":2,"q":"$(x+a)^2\\equiv x^2+10x+b$ for all values of $x$. Find $a$ and $b$.","o":["$a=10,\\ b=100$","$a=5,\\ b=10$","$a=10,\\ b=25$","$a=5,\\ b=25$"],"a":3,"x":"$(x+a)^2=x^2+2ax+a^2$. Matching $x$ terms gives $2a=10$, so $a=5$. Then $b=a^2=25$. The trap is forgetting the 2 in $2ax$.","w":["Took $a=10$ without halving, then squared it.","Found $a=5$ but copied 10 for $b$ instead of squaring $a$.","Took $a=10$ without halving, then used $5^2$. These two values do not match.",""]}
{"id":"gma-103","s":"maths","t":"A6-A7","lv":3,"q":"$n$ is any integer. Expand and simplify $(n+3)^2-(n-1)^2$. Which statement about the result is always true?","o":["It is always odd","It is always a multiple of 16","It is always a multiple of 8","It is always equal to $4n+8$"],"a":2,"x":"$(n^2+6n+9)-(n^2-2n+1)=8n+8=8(n+1)$, a multiple of 8 for every integer $n$. The trap is expanding $(n-1)^2$ wrongly and losing the $-2n$ term.","w":["$8(n+1)$ is a multiple of 8, so it is always even.","Only a multiple of 16 when $n+1$ is even. For $n=0$ the result is 8.","","The result is $8n+8$, not $4n+8$. This comes from an algebra slip in the expansion."]}
{"id":"gma-104","s":"maths","t":"A8-A10","lv":1,"q":"A straight line has the equation $y=3-2x$. What are its gradient and its $y$-intercept?","o":["Gradient 3, $y$-intercept $-2$","Gradient 2, $y$-intercept 3","Gradient $-2$, $y$-intercept 3","Gradient $-2$, $y$-intercept $-3$"],"a":2,"x":"Write it as $y=-2x+3$. The number multiplying $x$ is the gradient, $-2$. The constant is the $y$-intercept, 3. The trap is reading the numbers in the order they are written.","w":["Read the numbers in the order written, so the gradient and intercept are swapped.","Dropped the minus sign, so the gradient is $-2$, not 2.","","The gradient is right, but the intercept is $+3$. The sign was changed by mistake."]}
{"id":"gma-105","s":"maths","t":"A8-A10","lv":1,"q":"Which of these points lies on the line $y=3x-4$?","o":["$(2, 2)$","$(2, 10)$","$(0, 4)$","$(4, 12)$"],"a":0,"x":"Put $x=2$ into the equation: $y=3\\times2-4=2$. So $(2,2)$ is on the line. The trap is a wrong sign on the $-4$, or forgetting to subtract it.","w":["","Used $y=3x+4$ instead: $3\\times2+4=10$. The sign of the 4 was wrong.","The $y$-intercept is $-4$, so the point is $(0,-4)$. The sign was wrong.","Worked out $3\\times4=12$ but forgot to subtract 4. The correct $y$-value is 8."]}
{"id":"gma-106","s":"maths","t":"A8-A10","lv":2,"q":"A straight line passes through $(1, 5)$ and $(3, 1)$. What is the equation of the line?","o":["$y=2x+3$","$y=-2x+5$","$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{11}{2}$","$y=-2x+7$"],"a":3,"x":"Gradient $=\\frac{1-5}{3-1}=-2$. Use $(1,5)$: $5=-2+c$, so $c=7$. The line is $y=-2x+7$. Always substitute a point to find $c$.","w":["Lost the minus sign on the gradient, then $5=2+c$ gave $c=3$.","Gradient is right, but the $y$-value 5 was used as the intercept instead of substituting to find $c$.","Turned the gradient upside down. Gradient is change in $y$ over change in $x$, which is $-2$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-107","s":"maths","t":"A8-A10","lv":3,"q":"A line is perpendicular to $y=2x+1$ and passes through $(4, 3)$. What is its equation?","o":["$y=\\frac{1}{2}x+1$","$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+5$","$y=-2x+11$","$y=-\\frac{1}{2}x+3$"],"a":1,"x":"The perpendicular gradient is the negative reciprocal of 2, which is $-\\frac12$. Use $(4,3)$: $3=-2+c$, so $c=5$. The trap is only flipping or only negating the gradient.","w":["Flipped the gradient but did not make it negative. Perpendicular gradients multiply to $-1$.","","Only changed the sign of the gradient. It must be the negative reciprocal, $-\\frac12$.","Used the $y$-value 3 as the intercept instead of substituting $(4,3)$ to find $c$."]}
{"id":"gma-108","s":"maths","t":"A8-A10","lv":3,"q":"Which of these lines is perpendicular to the line $4x+2y=9$?","o":["$x-2y=6$","$x+2y=6$","$4x+2y=1$","$2x-y=5$"],"a":0,"x":"Rearrange $4x+2y=9$ to $y=-2x+4.5$, so the gradient is $-2$. The perpendicular gradient is $\\frac12$. $x-2y=6$ gives $y=\\frac12x-3$.","w":["","Gradient is $-\\frac12$. This only flipped $-2$ and kept the minus sign, so it is not perpendicular.","Gradient is $-2$, the same as the given line. This line is parallel, not perpendicular.","Gradient is 2. This only changed the sign and did not take the reciprocal."]}
{"id":"gma-109","s":"maths","t":"A11-A12","lv":1,"q":"Which equation could give a graph with two separate curved branches, one in the top right and one in the bottom left?","o":["$y=x^2$","$y=x^3$","$y=\\dfrac{1}{x}$","$y=-\\dfrac{1}{x}$"],"a":2,"x":"$y=\\frac{1}{x}$ is a reciprocal graph. It has two branches, in the positive/positive and negative/negative corners. The trap is mixing it up with a cubic, which is one continuous curve.","w":["This is a quadratic. It makes a single U-shaped curve, not two separate branches.","This is a cubic. It is one continuous curve through the origin, not two separate branches.","","The minus sign reflects the graph, so the branches are top left and bottom right, not top right and bottom left."]}
{"id":"gma-110","s":"maths","t":"A11-A12","lv":1,"q":"What are the coordinates of the turning point of $y=(x-3)^2+4$?","o":["$(3,4)$","$(-3,4)$","$(3,-4)$","$(4,3)$"],"a":0,"x":"The squared bracket is smallest (zero) when $x=3$. Then $y=4$. The turning point is $(3,4)$. The trap is taking the sign inside the bracket as it appears.","w":["","Sign slip: $(x-3)$ is zero when $x=3$, not $-3$.","The $+4$ gives a $y$-value of $+4$, so the sign has been changed wrongly.","The $x$ and $y$ values have been swapped round."]}
{"id":"gma-111","s":"maths","t":"A11-A12","lv":2,"q":"Find the coordinates of the minimum point of $y=x^2-4x+7$.","o":["$(-2,3)$","$(2,3)$","$(2,11)$","$(2,7)$"],"a":1,"x":"Complete the square: $x^2-4x+7=(x-2)^2-4+7=(x-2)^2+3$. The minimum is at $(2,3)$. The traps are the sign of the $x$-value and forgetting to subtract 4.","w":["Sign slip: $(x-2)^2$ is zero when $x=2$, not $-2$.","","Added 4 instead of subtracting it when completing the square, giving $+11$.","Used the $y$-intercept $7$ as the minimum, ignoring the $-4$ from completing the square."]}
{"id":"gma-112","s":"maths","t":"A11-A12","lv":2,"q":"A cubic graph has equation $y=(x+1)(x-2)(x-3)$. Where does it cross the $y$-axis?","o":["$(0,-6)$","$(0,-4)$","$(0,0)$","$(0,6)$"],"a":3,"x":"On the $y$-axis $x=0$. So $y=(1)(-2)(-3)=6$. The graph crosses at $(0,6)$. The trap is multiplying the roots $-1,2,3$ instead of substituting $x=0$.","w":["Multiplied the roots $-1$, $2$ and $3$ instead of putting $x=0$ into each bracket.","Added the numbers in the brackets, $1-2-3$, instead of multiplying them.","Thought the graph passes through the origin. It would only do that if one bracket was just $x$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-113","s":"maths","t":"A11-A12","lv":3,"q":"The graph of $y=x^2+bx+c$ has its minimum point at $(3,-2)$. Work out the value of $c$.","o":["$-2$","$9$","$7$","$11$"],"a":2,"x":"The equation is $y=(x-3)^2-2=x^2-6x+9-2=x^2-6x+7$. So $c=7$. The trap is giving the turning point's $y$-value as $c$, or forgetting to expand the bracket.","w":["This is the minimum $y$-value, not the $y$-intercept. The bracket must be expanded to find $c$.","Expanded $(x-3)^2$ to get $9$ but forgot to include the $-2$.","","Misread the form as $(x-3)^2+2$, so added $2$ to $9$ instead of subtracting it."]}
{"id":"gma-114","s":"maths","t":"A13","lv":3,"q":"The point $(1,4)$ is on the graph of $y=f(x)$. What are the coordinates of the matching point on the graph of $y=-f(x+2)$?","o":["$(3,-4)$","$(-1,4)$","$(-1,-4)$","$(1,-4)$"],"a":2,"x":"$f(x+2)$ moves the graph 2 left, so $(1,4)$ goes to $(-1,4)$. The minus sign outside reflects in the x-axis, so $(-1,-4)$. Do the shift and the reflection separately.","w":["The shift is the wrong way: $f(x+2)$ moves left, not right.","This does the shift but forgets the reflection in the x-axis from the minus sign.","","This does the reflection but forgets the 2 left shift from $f(x+2)$."]}
{"id":"gma-115","s":"maths","t":"A13","lv":3,"q":"Let $f(x)=x^2+3x$. The graph of $y=f(x)$ is reflected in the y-axis, then translated 2 units up. What is the equation of the new graph?","o":["$y=x^2+3x+2$","$y=x^2-3x+2$","$y=-x^2-3x+2$","$y=x^2-3x-2$"],"a":1,"x":"A reflection in the y-axis replaces $x$ with $-x$: $f(-x)=x^2-3x$. Moving 2 up adds 2 outside the bracket: $x^2-3x+2$. Reflecting in the x-axis would be $-f(x)$.","w":["This only adds 2 and skips the reflection, as if $x^2$ being symmetrical meant nothing changes.","","This is a reflection in the x-axis, $-f(x)$, not the y-axis.","The reflection is right, but $-2$ moves the graph down instead of up."]}
{"id":"gma-116","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":1,"q":"A distance-time graph is a straight line from (0, 0) to (2.5, 100). Time is in hours and distance is in km. What is the speed?","o":["250 km/h","40 km/h","0.025 km/h","97.5 km/h"],"a":1,"x":"Speed is the gradient: distance ÷ time = 100 ÷ 2.5 = 40 km/h. The trap is multiplying the two numbers or dividing the wrong way round.","w":["Multiplying 100 by 2.5 is wrong; speed is distance divided by time.","","Dividing time by distance turns the formula upside down; speed is distance ÷ time.","Subtracting the numbers is wrong; speed is the gradient, found by dividing."]}
{"id":"gma-117","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":1,"q":"A speed-time graph is a straight line from (0, 4) to (5, 19). Speed is in m/s and time is in seconds. What is the acceleration?","o":["3.8 m/s²","4.6 m/s²","15 m/s²","3 m/s²"],"a":3,"x":"Acceleration is the gradient: change in speed ÷ time = (19 − 4) ÷ 5 = 3 m/s². The trap is using the final speed instead of the change in speed.","w":["19 ÷ 5 ignores the starting speed; you must use the change in speed, 19 − 4.","Adding the speeds, (4 + 19) ÷ 5, is wrong; you need the change, so subtract.","15 is the change in speed but it has not been divided by the 5 seconds.",""]}
{"id":"gma-118","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":2,"q":"Sam cycles from home. A distance-time graph goes straight to (1, 20), is flat until 1.5, then goes straight to (2.5, 0). Time is in hours, distance in km. What is Sam's average speed overall?","o":["8 km/h","0 km/h","16 km/h","20 km/h"],"a":2,"x":"Total distance is 20 + 20 = 40 km. Total time is 2.5 hours, including the rest. 40 ÷ 2.5 = 16 km/h. Traps: using only the outward distance, or leaving out the rest.","w":["20 ÷ 2.5 only counts the outward trip; the ride back is distance travelled too.","0 confuses distance travelled with distance from home; Sam did travel 40 km.","","40 ÷ 2 leaves out the half hour rest; average speed uses the total time."]}
{"id":"gma-119","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":2,"q":"A speed-time graph rises in a straight line from 0 to 10 m/s in 4 s. It stays at 10 m/s for 6 s, then falls in a straight line to 0 in 2 s. How far does it travel?","o":["90 m","120 m","60 m","30 m"],"a":0,"x":"Distance is the area under the graph. Triangle: ½ × 4 × 10 = 20. Rectangle: 6 × 10 = 60. Triangle: ½ × 2 × 10 = 10. Total 90 m. The trap is treating it as one rectangle.","w":["","10 × 12 treats the whole journey as a rectangle at top speed, ignoring the slopes.","60 m is only the constant-speed part; the speeding up and slowing down also add distance.","30 m is only the two triangles; the constant-speed rectangle is missing."]}
{"id":"gma-120","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":3,"q":"A curved speed-time graph has speeds 0, 6, 8 and 12 m/s at 0, 2, 4 and 6 s. Use three trapezia of width 2 s to estimate the distance travelled.","o":["52 m","28 m","40 m","26 m"],"a":2,"x":"Area of each trapezium is ½ × 2 × (sum of parallel sides): 6 + 14 + 20 = 40 m. Traps: using rectangles with right-hand heights, or forgetting the strip width.","w":["2 × (6 + 8 + 12) uses rectangles with right-hand heights, which overestimates; trapezia use the average height.","2 × (0 + 6 + 8) uses rectangles with left-hand heights, which underestimates; trapezia use the average height.","","26 is just the speeds added; it ignores the strip width and the half in the trapezium formula."]}
{"id":"gma-121","s":"maths","t":"A16","lv":3,"q":"The line $L$ is the tangent to the circle $x^2+y^2=20$ at the point $(4,-2)$. At which point does $L$ cross the $y$-axis?","o":["$(0,10)$","$(0,-4)$","$(0,-10)$","$(0,6)$"],"a":2,"x":"The radius gradient is $-\\frac{1}{2}$, so the tangent gradient is $2$. Then $y+2=2(x-4)$ gives $y=2x-10$. At $x=0$, $y=-10$. The trap is using the wrong gradient or losing a sign.","w":["Sign slip in the intercept: $y+2=2(x-4)$ gives $-10$, not $+10$.","Used gradient $\\frac{1}{2}$, only changing the sign of the radius gradient and not flipping it.","","Used gradient $-2$, flipping the radius gradient but not changing its sign, so it is not perpendicular."]}
{"id":"gma-122","s":"maths","t":"A16","lv":3,"q":"The point $P$ is on the circle $x^2+y^2=25$. It has $x$-coordinate $-4$ and is below the $x$-axis. What is the equation of the tangent at $P$?","o":["$4x+3y=-25$","$4x+3y=25$","$3x+4y=-25$","$4x+3y=-7$"],"a":0,"x":"$P=(-4,-3)$ since $y^2=9$ and $y<0$. The radius gradient is $\\frac{3}{4}$, so the tangent gradient is $-\\frac{4}{3}$. Then $y+3=-\\frac{4}{3}(x+4)$ gives $4x+3y=-25$.","w":["","Ignored the negative signs of $P$; this tangent is at $(4,3)$, not $(-4,-3)$.","Swapped the coefficients; at $(-4,-3)$ the left side is $-24$, not $-25$.","Used the right gradient but the point $(-4,3)$; $P$ is below the axis, so $y=-3$."]}
{"id":"gma-123","s":"maths","t":"A17-A18","lv":1,"q":"Solve $4(x-3)=2x+6$.","o":["$x=3$","$x=9$","$x=4.5$","$x=18$"],"a":1,"x":"Expand: $4x-12=2x+6$. Subtract $2x$: $2x-12=6$. Add 12: $2x=18$. Divide by 2: $x=9$. The trap is forgetting to multiply $-3$ by 4.","w":["Sign slip: wrote $2x=12-6=6$, giving $x=3$. The 12 should be added to 6.","","Only multiplied $x$ by 4, giving $4x-3$. The $-3$ must also be multiplied by 4.","Stopped at $2x=18$ and forgot to divide by 2."]}
{"id":"gma-124","s":"maths","t":"A17-A18","lv":2,"q":"Solve $2x^2+7x+3=0$.","o":["$x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x=-3$","$x=\\frac{1}{2}$ or $x=3$","$x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ or $x=-1$","$x=-1$ or $x=-3$"],"a":0,"x":"Factorise: $(2x+1)(x+3)=0$. So $2x+1=0$ gives $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$, and $x+3=0$ gives $x=-3$. Remember to divide by 2 in the first bracket.","w":["","Wrong signs: the brackets $(2x-1)(x-3)$ expand to $2x^2-7x+3$.","Brackets swapped to $(2x+3)(x+1)$, which expands to $2x^2+5x+3$, not $2x^2+7x+3$.","Read $2x+1=0$ as $x=-1$, ignoring the 2 in front of $x$."]}
{"id":"gma-125","s":"maths","t":"A17-A18","lv":3,"q":"Use the quadratic formula to solve $x^2+4x-3=0$. Give your answers to 2 decimal places.","o":["$x=-1$ or $x=-3$","$x=4.65$ or $x=-0.65$","$x=0.65$ or $x=-4.65$","$x=-1.35$ or $x=-6.65$"],"a":2,"x":"Here $a=1$, $b=4$, $c=-3$. $x=\\frac{-4\\pm\\sqrt{16+12}}{2}=\\frac{-4\\pm\\sqrt{28}}{2}$. This gives 0.65 or $-4.65$. Note $-4ac=+12$.","w":["Used $16-12$ under the root because $c=-3$ was treated as $+3$. Then $\\sqrt{4}=2$.","Used $+b$ instead of $-b$ at the start of the formula.","","Divided only the square root by 2, not the whole top line."]}
{"id":"gma-126","s":"maths","t":"A17-A18","lv":3,"q":"Solve $x^2-6x+2=0$ by completing the square. Give your answers in surd form.","o":["$x=-3\\pm\\sqrt{7}$","$x=3\\pm\\sqrt{11}$","$x=3\\pm7$","$x=3\\pm\\sqrt{7}$"],"a":3,"x":"$x^2-6x+2=(x-3)^2-9+2=(x-3)^2-7$. So $(x-3)^2=7$ and $x=3\\pm\\sqrt{7}$. Two traps are the sign inside the bracket and the $-9$.","w":["Took the bracket as $(x+3)$, but half of $-6$ is $-3$, so the answer is $3\\pm\\sqrt7$.","Added 9 to 2 instead of subtracting it, giving $(x-3)^2=11$.","Forgot the square root: $(x-3)^2=7$ means $x-3=\\pm\\sqrt{7}$, not $\\pm7$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-127","s":"maths","t":"A19","lv":1,"q":"Solve $3x+y=14$ and $x+y=8$.","o":["$x=3,\\ y=5$","$x=5,\\ y=3$","$x=2,\\ y=6$","$x=6,\\ y=2$"],"a":0,"x":"The $y$ terms match and have the same sign, so subtract: $2x=6$, so $x=3$. Then $3+y=8$ gives $y=5$. Adding instead of subtracting would not remove $y$.","w":["","Swapped $x$ and $y$; it fits $x+y=8$ but $3(5)+3=18$, not 14.","Fits $x+y=8$ only; $3(2)+6=12$, not 14.","Fits $x+y=8$ only; $3(6)+2=20$, not 14."]}
{"id":"gma-128","s":"maths","t":"A19","lv":1,"q":"Solve $2x+3y=12$ and $5x-3y=9$. What is the value of $y$?","o":["$y=3$","$y=2$","$y=6$","$y=-2$"],"a":1,"x":"The $y$ terms are $+3y$ and $-3y$, so add: $7x=21$, so $x=3$. Then $6+3y=12$, so $3y=6$ and $y=2$. Do not forget to divide by 3.","w":["That is the value of $x$, not $y$; the question asks for $y$.","","Stopped at $3y=6$ and forgot to divide by 3.","Sign slip: worked out $6-12=-6$ instead of $12-6=6$."]}
{"id":"gma-129","s":"maths","t":"A19","lv":2,"q":"Solve $3x+2y=16$ and $x+4y=12$.","o":["$x=2,\\ y=4$","$x=6,\\ y=-1$","$x=4,\\ y=2$","$x=0,\\ y=3$"],"a":2,"x":"Multiply the first equation by 2: $6x+4y=32$. Subtract $x+4y=12$: $5x=20$, so $x=4$. Then $4+4y=12$ gives $y=2$. Multiply every term, including the 16.","w":["Swapped values; $3(2)+2(4)=14$, not 16.","Fits the first equation only; $6+4(-1)=2$, not 12.","","Fits the second equation only; $3(0)+2(3)=6$, not 16."]}
{"id":"gma-130","s":"maths","t":"A19","lv":2,"q":"2 adult tickets and 3 child tickets cost £34. 1 adult ticket and 2 child tickets cost £20. How much is one adult ticket?","o":["£6","£14","£10","£8"],"a":3,"x":"Let $a$ and $c$ be the prices. Double the second equation: $2a+4c=40$. Subtract the first: $c=6$. Then $a+12=20$, so $a=8$. £6 is the child price.","w":["That is the child ticket price; the question asks for an adult ticket.","That is $a+c$, one adult plus one child, not the adult price alone.","Used $a=20\\div2$, ignoring the child tickets in the second equation.",""]}
{"id":"gma-131","s":"maths","t":"A19","lv":3,"q":"Solve $y=x+2$ and $x^2+y^2=20$. Which pair of points gives both solutions $(x,y)$?","o":["$(-2,0)$ and $(4,6)$","$(2,4)$ and $(-4,-2)$","$(2,4)$ and $(4,2)$","$(2,4)$ and $(-4,2)$"],"a":1,"x":"Substitute: $x^2+(x+2)^2=20$, so $2x^2+4x-16=0$, then $x^2+2x-8=0$. This gives $x=2$ or $-4$. Use $y=x+2$ to find $y=4$ or $-2$.","w":["Factorised as $(x+2)(x-4)$, a sign slip; $(-2)^2+0^2=4$, not 20.","","$(4,2)$ fits $x^2+y^2=20$ but not $y=x+2$; the $y$ value was not found from the line.","$(-4,2)$ fits $x^2+y^2=20$ but not $y=x+2$, which gives $y=-2$."]}
{"id":"gma-132","s":"maths","t":"A20","lv":3,"q":"The equation $x^3+4x-7=0$ is rearranged to give an iteration formula $x_{n+1}=\\ldots$ Which formula is correct?","o":["$x_{n+1}=\\dfrac{x_n^3-7}{4}$","$x_{n+1}=\\dfrac{7-x_n^3}{4}$","$x_{n+1}=\\sqrt[3]{7+4x_n}$","$x_{n+1}=7-\\dfrac{x_n^3}{4}$"],"a":1,"x":"Start with $x^3+4x-7=0$. Then $4x=7-x^3$, so $x=\\frac{7-x^3}{4}$. The trap is a sign error or dividing only part of the right-hand side.","w":["Sign slip: $4x=7-x^3$, not $x^3-7$, so this is the negative of the correct formula.","","Sign slip: $x^3=7-4x$, not $7+4x$, so the cube root has the wrong sign inside.","Only $x^3$ was divided by 4. The 7 must be divided by 4 too, so brackets are needed."]}
{"id":"gma-133","s":"maths","t":"A20","lv":3,"q":"$f(x)=x^3-2x-5$ has a root near 2.1. Which pair of values proves that the root is 2.1 correct to 1 decimal place?","o":["$f(2)=-1$ and $f(2.1)=0.061$","$f(2.1)=0.061$ and $f(2.2)=1.248$","$f(2.05)=-0.485$ and $f(2.15)=0.638$","$f(2.1)=0.061$ and $f(2.15)=0.638$"],"a":2,"x":"For 1 d.p. you must show the root lies between 2.05 and 2.15. A sign change there proves it rounds to 2.1. The trap is using 2.0 and 2.1, which allows a root that rounds to 2.0.","w":["There is a sign change, but the root could be anywhere from 2.0 to 2.1 and so might round to 2.0.","Both values are positive, so there is no sign change and no root is shown in this interval.","","Both values are positive, so there is no sign change. It does not show a root between 2.1 and 2.15."]}
{"id":"gma-134","s":"maths","t":"A21-A22","lv":1,"q":"A rectangle has length $(x+4)$ cm and width $x$ cm. Its perimeter is 36 cm. Which equation can be used to find $x$?","o":["$2x+4=36$","$4x+8=36$","$x(x+4)=36$","$4x+4=36$"],"a":1,"x":"Perimeter is $2(x+4)+2x=4x+8$. Set this equal to 36. The trap is forgetting to double the 4, adding only two sides, or using area instead of perimeter.","w":["Added only one length and one width, which is half the perimeter.","","This is the area equation: length times width. The question gives the perimeter.","Expanded $2(x+4)$ as $2x+4$, forgetting to multiply the 4 by 2."]}
{"id":"gma-135","s":"maths","t":"A21-A22","lv":1,"q":"Which list shows all the integers $n$ that satisfy $-3 \\le n < 2$?","o":["$-2, -1, 0, 1, 2$","$-3, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2$","$-2, -1, 0, 1$","$-3, -2, -1, 0, 1$"],"a":3,"x":"$\\le$ includes $-3$. $<$ leaves out 2. So the integers are $-3, -2, -1, 0, 1$. The trap is mixing up which end is included.","w":["Left out $-3$ and included 2. The inclusive and strict signs were swapped.","Included 2, but $n<2$ does not allow $n=2$.","Left out $-3$. The sign $\\le$ means $-3$ is included.",""]}
{"id":"gma-136","s":"maths","t":"A21-A22","lv":2,"q":"Solve $3(x+2) > 4x-5$.","o":["$x<11$","$x>11$","$x<-1$","$x>-1$"],"a":0,"x":"Expand: $3x+6>4x-5$. Subtract $4x$ and 6: $-x>-11$. Dividing by $-1$ flips the sign, so $x<11$. The trap is not flipping the sign.","w":["","Divided by $-1$ but did not flip the inequality sign.","Moved the 6 with the wrong sign, getting $-x>1$, then flipped correctly.","Moved the 6 with the wrong sign and also did not flip the sign."]}
{"id":"gma-137","s":"maths","t":"A21-A22","lv":2,"q":"I think of a number $n$. Multiplying it by 5 and then subtracting 8 gives the same answer as doubling it and then adding 13. What is $n$?","o":["$3$","$\\frac{5}{3}$","$-7$","$7$"],"a":3,"x":"Form $5n-8=2n+13$. Subtract $2n$: $3n-8=13$. Add 8: $3n=21$, so $n=7$. The trap is mishandling the signs when collecting terms.","w":["Added $2n$ to $5n$ instead of subtracting, giving $7n=21$.","Did $13-8=5$ instead of adding 8 to both sides, giving $3n=5$.","Got the signs wrong on the numbers, so $3n=-21$ instead of $3n=21$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-138","s":"maths","t":"A21-A22","lv":3,"q":"Solve the inequality $x^2-3x-10<0$.","o":["$x<-2$ or $x>5$","$-5<x<2$","$-2<x<5$","$x<5$"],"a":2,"x":"Factorise: $(x+2)(x-5)<0$. The roots are $-2$ and 5. The graph is a U-shape, so it is below zero between the roots: $-2<x<5$. The trap is giving the region outside the roots.","w":["This is the region where the expression is positive (outside the roots), not negative.","Took the roots as $2$ and $-5$, with the signs of the brackets the wrong way round.","","Used only the root 5 and ignored the other root, $-2$."]}
{"id":"gma-139","s":"maths","t":"A23-A25","lv":1,"q":"A sequence starts 3, 4. Each next term is the sum of the two terms before it. What is the 6th term?","o":["18","47","29","19"],"a":2,"x":"The terms are 3, 4, 7, 11, 18, 29. Each term is the sum of the previous two. The trap is stopping one term early and giving the 5th term.","w":["This is the 5th term. Counting 3 and 4 as terms 1 and 2 gives 29 as the 6th.","This is the 7th term (18 + 29). Went one term too far.","","Kept adding 4 each time after 11. This sequence adds the previous two terms, not a fixed number."]}
{"id":"gma-140","s":"maths","t":"A23-A25","lv":1,"q":"The $n$th term of a sequence is $3n+1$. Which of these numbers is a term in the sequence?","o":["46","45","47","48"],"a":0,"x":"Solve $3n+1=46$ to get $3n=45$, so $n=15$, a whole number. For 45, 47 and 48, $n$ is not a whole number. The trap is forgetting the +1.","w":["","45 is a multiple of 3, but terms are one more than a multiple of 3. Forgot the +1.","$3n=46$ gives $n$ as a fraction, so 47 is not a term.","$3n=47$ gives $n$ as a fraction. 48 is a multiple of 3, but terms are one more than that."]}
{"id":"gma-141","s":"maths","t":"A23-A25","lv":2,"q":"A sequence starts 3, 6, 12, 24, ... Each term is double the one before. What is the 8th term?","o":["768","192","48","384"],"a":3,"x":"This is a geometric sequence (multiply by 2 each time): 3, 6, 12, 24, 48, 96, 192, 384. The 8th term is 384. The trap is listing one term too few or too many.","w":["This is $3\\times2^8$. The 8th term uses $2^7$ because the first term is not multiplied.","This is the 7th term. Stopped one term early when listing.","This is the 5th term. Stopped listing far too soon.",""]}
{"id":"gma-142","s":"maths","t":"A23-A25","lv":3,"q":"The first five terms of a quadratic sequence are 2, 7, 14, 23, 34. Find the $n$th term.","o":["$2n^2+3n-3$","$n^2+2n-1$","$n^2+2n+1$","$n^2+5n-4$"],"a":1,"x":"First differences are 5, 7, 9, 11. Second differences are 2, so use $n^2$. Subtracting $n^2$ leaves 1, 3, 5, 7, which is $2n-1$. So the answer is $n^2+2n-1$.","w":["Used the second difference 2 as the $n^2$ coefficient. It should be half of 2, which is 1.","","Gives 4 when $n=1$, not 2. The constant should be $-1$, not $+1$.","Used the first difference 5 as the coefficient of $n$. It gives 10 when $n=2$, not 7."]}
{"id":"gma-143","s":"maths","t":"A23-A25","lv":2,"q":"The $n$th term of a sequence is $n^2-3n$. Which term of the sequence is equal to 10?","o":["10","13","5","4"],"a":2,"x":"Solve $n^2-3n=10$. Try $n=5$: $25-15=10$. So the 5th term is 10. The trap is confusing the position $n$ with the value of the term.","w":["Put $n=10$, which gives 70. That is the 10th term, not the term equal to 10.","Added 3 to 10 instead of solving. The 13th term is $169-39=130$, not 10.","","The 4th term is $16-12=4$, not 10. Try the next value of $n$."]}
{"id":"gma-144","s":"maths","t":"R1-R2","lv":1,"q":"A rug has area 2.5 m². What is its area in cm²?","o":["250 cm²","2 500 cm²","25 000 cm²","250 000 cm²"],"a":2,"x":"1 m = 100 cm, so 1 m² = 100 × 100 = 10 000 cm². Then 2.5 × 10 000 = 25 000 cm². The trap is using 100 for area, as you would for length.","w":["Used 100 as the factor, which is the length factor. Area needs 100 × 100 = 10 000.","Used 1000 as the factor. 1 m² is 10 000 cm², not 1000 cm².","","Used 100 000 as the factor by miscounting zeros. 1 m² is 10 000 cm²."]}
{"id":"gma-145","s":"maths","t":"R1-R2","lv":2,"q":"A car travels at 72 km/h. What is this speed in m/s?","o":["20 m/s","1.2 m/s","259.2 m/s","200 m/s"],"a":0,"x":"72 km = 72 000 m and 1 hour = 3600 s. So 72 000 ÷ 3600 = 20 m/s. The trap is dividing by 60 only, or multiplying by 3.6 instead of dividing.","w":["","Divided by 60 only, which changes hours to minutes but not km to m or minutes to seconds.","Multiplied by 3.6 instead of dividing. Going from km/h to m/s makes the number smaller.","Divided 72 000 by 360, using the wrong number of seconds. An hour has 3600 seconds."]}
{"id":"gma-146","s":"maths","t":"R1-R2","lv":1,"q":"A water jug holds 3.6 litres. How many cm³ is this?","o":["360 cm³","36 000 cm³","3.6 cm³","3 600 cm³"],"a":3,"x":"1 litre = 1000 cm³, so 3.6 × 1000 = 3600 cm³. The trap is using the wrong power of 10, such as 100 or 10 000.","w":["Multiplied by 100. 1 litre is 1000 cm³, not 100 cm³.","Multiplied by 10 000. 1 litre is 1000 cm³, so there is one zero too many.","Thought 1 litre is 1 cm³. 1 litre is 1000 cm³.",""]}
{"id":"gma-147","s":"maths","t":"R1-R2","lv":2,"q":"On a map, a lake is 6 cm long. Its real length is 12 km. Write the map scale as 1 : n.","o":["1 : 20 000","1 : 200 000","1 : 2","1 : 2 000 000"],"a":1,"x":"12 km = 1 200 000 cm. The scale is 6 : 1 200 000. Divide by 6 to get 1 : 200 000. The trap is not changing km to cm first.","w":["Slip when changing km to cm: 12 km is 1 200 000 cm, so dividing by 6 gives 200 000, not 20 000.","","Worked out 12 ÷ 6 = 2 without changing units. The two lengths must be in the same unit first.","Added an extra zero when changing 12 km to cm. 12 km is 1 200 000 cm."]}
{"id":"gma-148","s":"maths","t":"R1-R2","lv":3,"q":"A metal has density 8.5 g/cm³. What is its density in kg/m³?","o":["8.5 kg/m³","850 kg/m³","8 500 kg/m³","8 500 000 kg/m³"],"a":2,"x":"1 m³ = 1 000 000 cm³, so 8.5 g/cm³ = 8 500 000 g/m³. Divide by 1000 to change g to kg: 8500 kg/m³. The trap is forgetting one of the two conversions.","w":["Left the number unchanged. Both the mass and volume units change, so the value changes too.","Used 100 for cm³ to m³ and ignored g to kg. 1 m³ is 1 000 000 cm³.","","Changed cm³ to m³ but forgot to change g to kg. Divide by 1000 to get kg."]}
{"id":"gma-149","s":"maths","t":"R3-R8","lv":1,"q":"Red and blue counters are in the ratio $3:5$. What fraction of all the counters are blue?","o":["$\\frac{3}{8}$","$\\frac{5}{3}$","$\\frac{5}{8}$","$\\frac{3}{5}$"],"a":2,"x":"Total parts $=3+5=8$. Blue is 5 of the 8 parts, so the fraction is $\\frac{5}{8}$. The trap is using a part, not the whole, as the denominator.","w":["This is the fraction that are red, not blue.","This compares blue to red (part to part), not blue to all counters.","","This compares red to blue (part to part), so the denominator ignores the total of 8 parts."]}
{"id":"gma-150","s":"maths","t":"R3-R8","lv":1,"q":"A ribbon is $40$ cm long. Another ribbon is $1.2$ m long. Write the ratio of the first length to the second in its simplest form.","o":["$1:3$","$100:3$","$10:3$","$3:1$"],"a":0,"x":"Use the same units: $1.2$ m $=120$ cm. Then $40:120$, and dividing by 40 gives $1:3$. The trap is simplifying without converting units.","w":["","Ignores the units and uses $40:1.2$ directly.","Converts $1.2$ m to $12$ cm by mistake, giving $40:12$.","Ratio is the wrong way round; the first length is the shorter one."]}
{"id":"gma-151","s":"maths","t":"R3-R8","lv":2,"q":"The ratio of boys to girls in a club is $4:7$. There are $28$ girls. How many students are in the club altogether?","o":["$16$","$32$","$44$","$308$"],"a":2,"x":"$7$ parts $=28$, so $1$ part $=4$. Boys $=4\\times4=16$. Total $=28+16=44$. The trap is stopping at the number of boys.","w":["This is only the number of boys, not the total.","Adds the ratio number 4 to 28 instead of finding the value of 4 parts.","","Multiplies 28 by the total of 11 parts instead of finding one part first."]}
{"id":"gma-152","s":"maths","t":"R3-R8","lv":2,"q":"Amir and Beth share money in the ratio $7:3$. Amir gets £24 more than Beth. How much does Amir get?","o":["£18","£42","£56","£60"],"a":1,"x":"The difference is $7-3=4$ parts, so $4$ parts $=$ £24 and $1$ part $=$ £6. Amir gets $7\\times6=42$, so £42. The trap is treating £24 as one person's share.","w":["This is Beth's share ($3\\times6$), not Amir's.","","Treats £24 as Beth's 3 parts, so 1 part is £8, but £24 is the difference.","This is the total for both people ($10\\times6$), not Amir's share."]}
{"id":"gma-153","s":"maths","t":"R3-R8","lv":3,"q":"Sam and Tia have sweets in the ratio $3:5$. Sam gives Tia $6$ sweets. The ratio is now $1:3$. How many sweets do they have altogether?","o":["$6$","$24$","$36$","$48$"],"a":3,"x":"Let Sam have $3k$ and Tia $5k$. Then $3(3k-6)=5k+6$, so $4k=24$ and $k=6$. The total is unchanged: $8k=48$. Check: $18:30$ becomes $12:36=1:3$. The trap is stopping at $k$ or $4k$.","w":["This is the value of one part, $k$, not the total.","This is the value of $4k$ from the equation, not $8k$.","This is Tia's amount after receiving 6 sweets, not the total.",""]}
{"id":"gma-154","s":"maths","t":"R9","lv":1,"q":"A bike costs £250. Its price rises to £280. What is the percentage increase?","o":["10.7%","30%","12%","112%"],"a":2,"x":"The increase is £30. Divide by the original price, not the new one: $30\\div250\\times100=12\\%$. Dividing by £280 is the common trap.","w":["Divided the increase by the new price (£280) instead of the original price.","Gave the £30 change as a percentage without dividing by the original price.","","Gave the new price as a percentage of the old price (112%), not the increase."]}
{"id":"gma-155","s":"maths","t":"R9","lv":1,"q":"£800 is invested for 4 years at 3% per year simple interest. How much interest is earned?","o":["£24","£100.41","£896","£96"],"a":3,"x":"Simple interest is the same every year. 3% of £800 is £24. Over 4 years, $24\\times4=96$, so £96. Do not use compound growth.","w":["This is the interest for one year only, not 4 years.","Used compound interest, adding interest to the amount each year, but the question says simple interest.","This is the total amount in the account, not the interest earned.",""]}
{"id":"gma-156","s":"maths","t":"R9","lv":2,"q":"In a sale, a jacket is reduced by 25%. The sale price is £60. What was the original price?","o":["£80","£75","£85","£45"],"a":0,"x":"The sale price is 75% of the original. Divide by 0.75: $60\\div0.75=80$. The trap is adding 25% of £60 to the sale price.","w":["","Found 25% of £60 (£15) and added it, but the 25% is of the original price.","Added £25 to the sale price instead of finding the percentage.","Took 25% off £60, which reduces it again instead of reversing the sale."]}
{"id":"gma-157","s":"maths","t":"R9","lv":2,"q":"A £40 item is reduced to £34. Later it goes back up from £34 to £40. What is the percentage increase from £34 to £40?","o":["15%","17.6%","6%","85%"],"a":1,"x":"The increase is £6. Divide by the starting price of this change, £34: $6\\div34\\times100\\approx17.6\\%$. It is not the same as the 15% decrease.","w":["Divided by £40, giving the decrease from £40 to £34, but this change starts at £34.","","This is the £6 change written as a percentage, without dividing by the original value.","This is £34 as a percentage of £40, not the percentage increase."]}
{"id":"gma-158","s":"maths","t":"R9","lv":3,"q":"A coat is reduced by 20%. The sale price is then reduced by a further 10%. The final price is £72. What was the original price?","o":["£90","£102.86","£100","£93.60"],"a":2,"x":"The multipliers are 0.8 and 0.9, so the original is multiplied by 0.72. Divide: $72\\div0.72=100$. The trap is treating it as one 30% reduction.","w":["Only reversed the 20% reduction ($72\\div0.8$) and ignored the extra 10%.","Treated it as one 30% reduction and divided by 0.7, but the second 10% is of a smaller price.","","Multiplied by 1.3 to add 30% back, which is not how to reverse a percentage reduction."]}
{"id":"gma-159","s":"maths","t":"R10-R11","lv":1,"q":"A train travels 150 km in 2 hours 30 minutes. What is its average speed?","o":["65.2 km/h","75 km/h","375 km/h","60 km/h"],"a":3,"x":"Speed = distance ÷ time. 2 hours 30 minutes is 2.5 hours, so $150\\div2.5=60$ km/h. The trap is writing 2 hours 30 minutes as 2.3 hours.","w":["Treated 2 hours 30 minutes as 2.3 hours, but 30 minutes is 0.5 hours.","Divided by 2 and ignored the 30 minutes.","Multiplied distance by time instead of dividing distance by time.",""]}
{"id":"gma-160","s":"maths","t":"R10-R11","lv":1,"q":"A force of 240 N acts on an area of 0.5 m². What is the pressure?","o":["120 N/m²","480 N/m²","0.0021 N/m²","239.5 N/m²"],"a":1,"x":"Pressure = force ÷ area $=240\\div0.5=480$ N/m². Dividing by 0.5 doubles the number. The trap is multiplying by 0.5 instead.","w":["Multiplied force by area instead of dividing, but dividing by 0.5 makes the answer bigger.","","Divided area by force, the wrong way round.","Subtracted the area from the force instead of dividing."]}
{"id":"gma-161","s":"maths","t":"R10-R11","lv":2,"q":"4 builders take 9 days to build a wall. All builders work at the same rate. How long would 6 builders take?","o":["13.5 days","7 days","6 days","54 days"],"a":2,"x":"This is inverse proportion. Total work is $4\\times9=36$ builder-days. Then $36\\div6=6$ days. More builders means fewer days, so the answer must be below 9.","w":["Used direct proportion, so more builders gave more days instead of fewer.","Subtracted 2 for the 2 extra builders, but the change is not additive.","","Multiplied 9 by 6, but more builders should reduce the time, not increase it."]}
{"id":"gma-162","s":"maths","t":"R10-R11","lv":2,"q":"A liquid has density 0.9 g/cm³ and mass 450 g. What is its volume?","o":["500 cm³","405 cm³","0.002 cm³","50 cm³"],"a":0,"x":"Volume = mass ÷ density $=450\\div0.9=500$ cm³. Dividing by a number less than 1 gives a bigger answer. The trap is multiplying mass by density, which gives 405.","w":["","Multiplied mass by density instead of dividing mass by density.","Divided density by mass, the wrong way round.","Decimal slip: dividing by 0.9 is the same as multiplying by $\\frac{10}{9}$, so the answer is 500, not 50."]}
{"id":"gma-163","s":"maths","t":"R10-R11","lv":3,"q":"A cyclist rides 12 km at 24 km/h, then a further 12 km at 8 km/h. What is the average speed for the whole journey?","o":["16 km/h","12 km/h","6 km/h","32 km/h"],"a":1,"x":"Times: $12\\div24=0.5$ h and $12\\div8=1.5$ h. Total time is 2 h. Total distance is 24 km. Average speed $=24\\div2=12$ km/h. The trap is averaging the two speeds.","w":["Found the mean of 24 and 8, ignoring that more time is spent at the slower speed.","","Used only 12 km as the total distance instead of 24 km.","Added the two speeds instead of using total distance ÷ total time."]}
{"id":"gma-164","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":1,"q":"Triangles ABC and DEF are similar. AB = 6 cm matches DE = 9 cm. BC = 10 cm. What is the length of EF?","o":["13 cm","15 cm","6.7 cm","25 cm"],"a":1,"x":"The scale factor is $9\\div6=1.5$. Multiply the matching side: $10\\times1.5=15$ cm. Adding the difference of 3 cm is the trap: similar shapes use multiplying, not adding.","w":["Added the difference 3 cm to 10; similar shapes scale by multiplying, not adding.","","Divided 10 by 1.5, so used the scale factor the wrong way round.","Added 6, 9 and 10 instead of using the scale factor."]}
{"id":"gma-165","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":1,"q":"A straight line on a distance-time graph passes through (0, 0) and (5, 20). Distance is in km and time is in hours. What does the gradient show, and what is it?","o":["Distance, 20 km","Speed, 0.25 km/h","Speed, 4 km/h","Acceleration, 4 km/h²"],"a":2,"x":"Gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time $=20\\div5=4$. On a distance-time graph this is speed, so 4 km/h. Dividing time by distance gives the wrong 0.25.","w":["20 km is just the final distance read off the graph, not the gradient.","Divided time by distance ($5\\div20$), so turned the gradient upside down.","","Acceleration is the gradient of a speed-time graph, not a distance-time graph."]}
{"id":"gma-166","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":2,"q":"Two similar bottles have heights 10 cm and 15 cm. The label on the smaller bottle has area 80 cm². What is the area of the label on the larger bottle?","o":["120 cm²","270 cm²","35.6 cm²","180 cm²"],"a":3,"x":"The length scale factor is $15\\div10=1.5$. Areas scale by $1.5^2=2.25$. So $80\\times2.25=180$ cm². Using 1.5 alone is the trap.","w":["Multiplied by the length factor 1.5 only; area needs the factor squared.","Cubed the scale factor, which is for volume, not area.","Divided by 2.25 instead of multiplying; the larger bottle has the larger label.",""]}
{"id":"gma-167","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":2,"q":"Two similar jugs hold 250 ml and 2000 ml. The smaller jug is 8 cm tall. How tall is the larger jug?","o":["16 cm","64 cm","22.6 cm","32 cm"],"a":0,"x":"Volume scale factor is $2000\\div250=8$. The length factor is the cube root: $\\sqrt[3]{8}=2$. Height is $8\\times2=16$ cm. Using 8 as a length factor is the trap.","w":["","Multiplied the height by the volume factor 8; lengths need the cube root.","Used the square root of 8 as the length factor; volume needs the cube root.","Multiplied by the area factor 4; height is a length, so needs the length factor 2."]}
{"id":"gma-168","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":3,"q":"Two similar solids have surface areas 18 cm² and 50 cm². The smaller solid has volume 54 cm³. What is the volume of the larger solid?","o":["90 cm³","250 cm³","150 cm³","750 cm³"],"a":1,"x":"Area ratio $18:50=9:25$, so length ratio is $3:5$. Volume ratio is $27:125$. Volume $=54\\times\\frac{125}{27}=250$ cm³. Skipping the square root step is the trap.","w":["Used the length factor $5/3$ only; volume needs the cube.","","Used the area factor $50/18$ for volume, skipping the step to lengths and cubing.","Mixed up the factors, using $125/9$ instead of $125/27$."]}
{"id":"gma-169","s":"maths","t":"R15","lv":3,"q":"Water fills a tank. A tangent to the volume-time graph at 6 min passes through (2, 30) and (10, 110). Volume is in litres, time in minutes. Estimate the rate of flow in litres per second.","o":["10 L/s","0.17 L/s","600 L/s","1.33 L/s"],"a":1,"x":"The gradient of the tangent is $\\frac{110-30}{10-2}=10$ litres per minute. There are 60 seconds in a minute, so divide by 60: about 0.17 L/s. The trap is forgetting to change the time unit.","w":["This is the rate in litres per minute; the time unit was not changed to seconds.","","Multiplied by 60 instead of dividing; fewer litres flow each second than each minute.","Divided only the rise (80) by 60 and ignored the time change of 8 minutes."]}
{"id":"gma-170","s":"maths","t":"R15","lv":3,"q":"A drink cools. A tangent to the temperature-time graph at $t=10$ min passes through (0, 80) and (20, 50). Temperature is in °C. Which statement about $t=10$ is correct?","o":["Rising at 1.5 °C per minute","Falling at 6.5 °C per minute","Falling at 0.67 °C per minute","Falling at 1.5 °C per minute"],"a":3,"x":"The tangent gradient is $\\frac{50-80}{20-0}=-1.5$. The negative sign means the temperature is falling at 1.5 °C per minute. The traps are ignoring the sign and turning the gradient upside down.","w":["The gradient is negative, so the temperature is falling, not rising; the sign was ignored.","Used the temperature at 10 min (65) divided by 10, which is not the gradient of the tangent.","Inverted the gradient: it is change in temperature over change in time, not the reverse.",""]}
{"id":"gma-171","s":"maths","t":"R16","lv":1,"q":"A machine loses 8% of its value each year. Which number do you multiply by to find its value after one year?","o":["0.08","0.8","0.92","1.08"],"a":2,"x":"A decrease of 8% leaves 92% of the value, so the multiplier is 1 − 0.08 = 0.92. The trap is using the percentage itself, or adding 8% instead of taking it off.","w":["0.08 is only the 8% that is lost. It would leave just 8% of the value, not 92%.","0.8 mistakes 8% for 80%. It converts the percentage to a decimal wrongly.","","1.08 is the multiplier for an 8% increase. It adds the 8% when the value is meant to go down."]}
{"id":"gma-172","s":"maths","t":"R16","lv":1,"q":"A car is worth £12 000 and loses 15% of its value each year. Which expression gives its value after 4 years?","o":["$12000\\times1.15^4$","$12000\\times0.15^4$","$12000\\times(1-0.15\\times4)$","$12000\\times0.85^4$"],"a":3,"x":"Each year the value is multiplied by 0.85. After 4 years that is $12000\\times0.85^4$. The traps are using 1.15, using 0.15, or taking 15% off the original value four times.","w":["1.15 is the multiplier for growth. The car is losing value, so this is the wrong multiplier.","0.15 is the amount lost each year, not the amount kept. The multiplier should be 0.85.","This takes 15% of the original value off four times, which is simple decrease, not compound decay.",""]}
{"id":"gma-173","s":"maths","t":"R16","lv":2,"q":"The population of a town is 20 000. It grows by 3% each year. What is the population after 2 years, to the nearest whole number?","o":["21 200","21 218","21 855","18 818"],"a":1,"x":"Multiply by 1.03 twice: $20000\\times1.03^2=21218$. Adding 3% of 20 000 twice gives 21 200, which ignores growth on growth.","w":["This adds 3% of 20 000 each year (600 twice). Growth is compound, so the second year's 3% is of the larger number.","","This uses 3 years of growth, $1.03^3$, not 2 years.","This uses 0.97, a decrease of 3%, but the population is growing."]}
{"id":"gma-174","s":"maths","t":"R16","lv":2,"q":"A colony starts with 400 bacteria. The number is multiplied by 1.5 every hour. How many bacteria are there after 3 hours?","o":["1800","900","2025","1350"],"a":3,"x":"Multiply by 1.5 three times: $400\\times1.5^3=400\\times3.375=1350$. The trap is multiplying by $1.5\\times3$, which gives 1800 instead of using a power.","w":["This is $400\\times1.5\\times3$. It multiplies by 3 instead of using 1.5 three times.","This is $400\\times1.5^2$, which is only 2 hours of growth.","This is $400\\times1.5^4$, which is 4 hours of growth, not 3.",""]}
{"id":"gma-175","s":"maths","t":"R16","lv":3,"q":"An investment earns 5% compound interest per year. After 2 years it is worth £2756.25. How much was originally invested?","o":["£2500","£2625","£2505.68","£2487.52"],"a":0,"x":"After 2 years the amount is the original × $1.05^2$ = original × 1.1025. So divide: $2756.25\\div1.1025=2500$. The trap is dividing by 1.05 only once, or by 1.1.","w":["","Dividing by 1.05 once undoes only one year of interest, not two.","Dividing by 1.1 treats 2 years as simple interest of 10%. Compound interest gives 1.1025.","Multiplying by 0.9025 (0.95²) is wrong. A 5% decrease is not the reverse of a 5% increase."]}
{"id":"gma-176","s":"maths","t":"G1-G2","lv":1,"q":"A straight line joins two points on the circumference of a circle. It does not have to pass through the centre. What is this line called?","o":["A diameter","A chord","A tangent","An arc"],"a":1,"x":"A chord joins any two points on the circumference. A diameter is a special chord that passes through the centre. Do not mix up chord and diameter.","w":["A diameter must pass through the centre, so this is the chord mix-up.","","A tangent touches the circle at just one point and does not join two points on it.","An arc is a curved part of the circumference, not a straight line."]}
{"id":"gma-177","s":"maths","t":"G1-G2","lv":1,"q":"The perpendicular bisector of $PQ$ meets $PQ$ at $M$. $PQ = 9.4$ cm. Which statement is correct?","o":["$MQ = 9.4$ cm and the lines meet at $90^\\circ$","$MQ = 4.7$ cm and the lines meet at $45^\\circ$","$MQ = 4.7$ cm and the lines meet at $90^\\circ$","$MQ = 4.7$ cm and the lines meet at $180^\\circ$"],"a":2,"x":"A perpendicular bisector cuts the line in half, so $MQ = 9.4 \\div 2 = 4.7$ cm. It meets the line at right angles, so the angle is $90^\\circ$. Do not confuse it with an angle bisector.","w":["The line is bisected, so $MQ$ is half of $PQ$. This option forgets to halve.","$45^\\circ$ comes from halving a right angle. Perpendicular means the lines meet at exactly $90^\\circ$.","","$180^\\circ$ is the angle along the straight line $PQ$, not the angle between the two lines."]}
{"id":"gma-178","s":"maths","t":"G1-G2","lv":2,"q":"Line $AB$ is 5 cm long. Which description gives the boundary of all points exactly 2 cm from the line segment $AB$?","o":["Two circles of radius 2 cm, one centred at each end of $AB$","Two straight lines parallel to $AB$, each 2 cm away, with nothing at the ends","A rectangle with square corners, extending 2 cm beyond $AB$ all round","Two parallel lines 2 cm from $AB$, joined by a semicircle of radius 2 cm at each end"],"a":3,"x":"Along the sides the points are 2 cm from the line, giving parallel lines. Beyond each end, points 2 cm from that end make a semicircle. Square corners are a common slip.","w":["This only uses the end points. It misses the points 2 cm from the middle of the line.","The ends are left open. Points beyond the ends must also be 2 cm from the nearest end point.","Square corners are further than 2 cm from the end points. The ends must be rounded.",""]}
{"id":"gma-179","s":"maths","t":"G1-G2","lv":2,"q":"$AB = 10$ cm. Point $P$ is on $AB$. $P$ is closer to $B$ than to $A$, and is less than 7 cm from $A$. Which distance $AP$ is possible?","o":["6 cm","4 cm","5 cm","8 cm"],"a":0,"x":"Closer to $B$ than $A$ means $AP$ is more than 5 cm, past the perpendicular bisector. Less than 7 cm from $A$ means $AP < 7$. So $5 < AP < 7$, and only 6 cm fits.","w":["","4 cm is closer to $A$ than to $B$. The inequality has been reversed.","5 cm is the midpoint, equidistant from $A$ and $B$, so it is not closer to $B$.","8 cm is closer to $B$ but is further than 7 cm from $A$. The second condition is ignored."]}
{"id":"gma-180","s":"maths","t":"G1-G2","lv":3,"q":"Garden $ABCD$ is a rectangle with $AB = 30$ m and $AD = 20$ m. A dog is tied at $A$ by a 20 m rope and stays inside the garden. To the nearest m², what area can it reach?","o":["1257 m²","600 m²","314 m²","628 m²"],"a":2,"x":"The fence at $A$ cuts the circle to a quarter circle of radius 20 m. Area $=\\frac{1}{4}\\times\\pi\\times 20^2 = 100\\pi \\approx 314$ m². Do not use the full circle.","w":["This is the full circle $\\pi \\times 20^2$. The dog cannot go outside the garden.","This is the whole garden, $30 \\times 20$. The rope cannot reach all of it.","","This is a half circle. At a corner only a quarter of the circle is inside the garden."]}
{"id":"gma-181","s":"maths","t":"G3-G4","lv":1,"q":"Two parallel lines are crossed by a third straight line. One of the angles between the parallel lines is $65^\\circ$. What is the size of its co-interior (allied) angle?","o":["$65^\\circ$","$115^\\circ$","$25^\\circ$","$295^\\circ$"],"a":1,"x":"Co-interior angles add up to $180^\\circ$. $180-65=115^\\circ$. The trap is mixing them up with alternate angles, which are equal.","w":["This is an alternate or corresponding angle, which is equal. Co-interior angles add to $180^\\circ$.","","This uses $90-65$. Co-interior angles add to $180^\\circ$, not $90^\\circ$.","This uses $360-65$. Co-interior angles add to $180^\\circ$, not $360^\\circ$."]}
{"id":"gma-182","s":"maths","t":"G3-G4","lv":2,"q":"ABCD is a parallelogram. Angle $A=(2x+10)^\\circ$ and the adjacent angle $B=(3x-20)^\\circ$. Work out the size of angle $A$.","o":["$94^\\circ$","$38^\\circ$","$70^\\circ$","$86^\\circ$"],"a":3,"x":"Adjacent angles in a parallelogram add up to $180^\\circ$. $5x-10=180$, so $x=38$. Angle $A=2\\times38+10=86^\\circ$. The trap is stopping at $x$.","w":["This is angle $B$, not angle $A$. Substitute $x=38$ into $2x+10$.","This is the value of $x$, not the angle. Put it back into $2x+10$.","This sets the adjacent angles equal. Opposite angles are equal; adjacent angles add to $180^\\circ$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-183","s":"maths","t":"G3-G4","lv":2,"q":"Each interior angle of a regular polygon is $156^\\circ$. How many sides does the polygon have?","o":["$24$","$12$","$15$","$13$"],"a":2,"x":"Exterior angle $=180-156=24^\\circ$. Number of sides $=360\\div24=15$. The trap is giving the exterior angle or a half-finished answer.","w":["This is the exterior angle, $180-156$. You still need to divide $360$ by it.","A regular 12-sided shape has interior angle $150^\\circ$, not $156^\\circ$.","","This is $n-2$ from $(n-2)\\times180=156n$. Add $2$ to get the number of sides."]}
{"id":"gma-184","s":"maths","t":"G3-G4","lv":3,"q":"ABCDE is a regular pentagon. Triangle ABC is drawn by joining A to C. Work out the size of angle ACD.","o":["$72^\\circ$","$36^\\circ$","$108^\\circ$","$54^\\circ$"],"a":0,"x":"Interior angle $=108^\\circ$. Triangle ABC is isosceles, so angle $BCA=(180-108)\\div2=36^\\circ$. Angle $ACD=108-36=72^\\circ$. The trap is stopping at $36^\\circ$.","w":["","This is angle $BCA$ only. Angle $ACD$ is the rest of angle $BCD$, so subtract from $108^\\circ$.","This is the whole interior angle $BCD$, not the part of it inside angle $ACD$.","This halves $108^\\circ$. Triangle ABC is isosceles with apex $108^\\circ$, so its base angles are $36^\\circ$."]}
{"id":"gma-185","s":"maths","t":"G5-G6","lv":1,"q":"Which fact is NOT enough, on its own, to prove that two triangles are congruent?","o":["The three pairs of sides are equal","The three pairs of angles are equal","Two pairs of sides and the angle between them are equal","Right angle, hypotenuse and one other side are equal (RHS)"],"a":1,"x":"Equal angles (AAA) only show the triangles are the same shape. One could be an enlargement of the other. SSS, SAS, ASA and RHS all fix the size too.","w":["SSS is a valid congruence rule, so this is enough.","","SAS is a valid rule. The angle is between the two sides, so this is enough.","RHS is a valid rule for right-angled triangles, so this is enough."]}
{"id":"gma-186","s":"maths","t":"G5-G6","lv":1,"q":"A right-angled triangle has hypotenuse 13 cm and one shorter side 5 cm. Work out the length of the other shorter side.","o":["8 cm","13.9 cm","144 cm","12 cm"],"a":3,"x":"The hypotenuse is known, so subtract: $13^2-5^2=169-25=144$. Then $\\sqrt{144}=12$ cm. Adding the squares is the trap.","w":["Subtracted the lengths (13 − 5) instead of the squares.","Added the squares, as if finding the hypotenuse.","Forgot the square root: 144 is the side squared.",""]}
{"id":"gma-187","s":"maths","t":"G5-G6","lv":2,"q":"A ladder 2.5 m long leans against a vertical wall. Its foot is 0.7 m from the wall on level ground. How high up the wall does it reach?","o":["2.6 m","2.4 m","1.8 m","5.76 m"],"a":1,"x":"The ladder is the hypotenuse. Height$^2=2.5^2-0.7^2=6.25-0.49=5.76$, so height $=2.4$ m. Do not add the squares.","w":["Added the squares, which treats the ladder as a shorter side.","","Subtracted lengths (2.5 − 0.7) instead of squares.","Forgot the square root at the end."]}
{"id":"gma-188","s":"maths","t":"G5-G6","lv":2,"q":"An isosceles triangle has two equal sides of 10 cm and a base of 12 cm. Work out its area.","o":["60 cm²","40 cm²","48 cm²","96 cm²"],"a":2,"x":"The height splits the base into 6 cm and 6 cm. Height$^2=10^2-6^2=64$, so height is 8 cm. Area $=\\tfrac12\\times12\\times8=48$ cm². Using the sloping side as the height is the trap.","w":["Used the sloping side 10 cm as the height, not the perpendicular height.","Used the sloping side 10 cm as the base instead of the 12 cm base.","","Found the height correctly but forgot the half in the area formula."]}
{"id":"gma-189","s":"maths","t":"G5-G6","lv":3,"q":"ABCD is a parallelogram. The diagonal AC splits it into triangles ABC and CDA. Which reason correctly proves that the two triangles are congruent?","o":["AB = CD, BC = DA and AC is shared, so SSS","All three angles are equal in both triangles, so AAA","AB = CD and AC is shared, so SAS","Angle B = angle D and AC is shared, so ASA"],"a":0,"x":"Opposite sides of a parallelogram are equal and AC is common to both triangles, so all three sides match: SSS. AAA never proves congruence, and the other two lack the needed matching parts.","w":["","AAA shows only the same shape, not the same size, so it is not a congruence rule.","SAS needs an equal angle between the two sides, and no angle is given here.","One angle pair and one shared side are not enough. ASA needs two angles and the side between them."]}
{"id":"gma-190","s":"maths","t":"G7-G8","lv":2,"q":"The point $(4,3)$ is reflected in the line $x=1$. What are the coordinates of its image?","o":["$(-4,3)$","$(-3,3)$","$(-2,3)$","$(4,-1)$"],"a":2,"x":"The point is 3 units right of $x=1$. Its image is 3 units left of the line, at $x=1-3=-2$. The y-coordinate stays 3. The trap is reflecting in the y-axis instead.","w":["That is a reflection in the y-axis, not in the line $x=1$.","This uses the distance from the y-axis (4), not the distance from the line $x=1$ (3).","","That is a reflection in the line $y=1$, which changes the y-coordinate instead of the x-coordinate."]}
{"id":"gma-192","s":"maths","t":"G7-G8","lv":1,"q":"A shape is translated. The point $A(1,5)$ moves to $A'(-2,1)$. Which column vector describes the translation?","o":["$\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\4\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-4\\\\-3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-1\\\\6\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\-4\\end{pmatrix}$"],"a":3,"x":"Subtract start from end. x: $-2-1=-3$. y: $1-5=-4$. The vector is $\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\-4\\end{pmatrix}$. The trap is subtracting the wrong way round, which reverses the direction.","w":["This is the vector from $A'$ to $A$, so the direction is reversed.","The x and y movements are swapped. The top number must be the x-movement.","This adds the coordinates instead of finding the change from start to end.",""]}
{"id":"gma-194","s":"maths","t":"G7-G8","lv":3,"q":"The point $(2,1)$ is reflected in the x-axis. The image is then rotated $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise about $(0,0)$. What are the final coordinates?","o":["$(-1,2)$","$(-1,-2)$","$(1,2)$","$(1,-2)$"],"a":2,"x":"Reflect in the x-axis: $(2,1)\\to(2,-1)$. Rotate $90^\\circ$ anticlockwise: $(x,y)\\to(-y,x)$, so $(2,-1)\\to(1,2)$. The trap is doing the steps in the wrong order.","w":["This only rotates $(2,1)$ and misses the reflection.","This does the rotation first and the reflection second, so the order is wrong.","","This rotates $(2,1)$ clockwise and misses the reflection."]}
{"id":"gma-195","s":"maths","t":"G9-G10","lv":1,"q":"A chord is drawn across a circle but not through the centre. What is the name for the smaller region between the chord and the arc?","o":["A sector","A semicircle","A segment","A tangent"],"a":2,"x":"A segment is the region between a chord and an arc. A sector is bounded by two radii and an arc. Do not mix up segment and sector.","w":["A sector is bounded by two radii and an arc, not by a chord; this mixes up sector and segment.","A semicircle only happens when the chord is a diameter, and this chord misses the centre.","","A tangent is a line that touches the circle at one point; it is not a region."]}
{"id":"gma-197","s":"maths","t":"G9-G10","lv":3,"q":"$ABCD$ is a cyclic quadrilateral. Angle $DAB=3x$ and angle $BCD=2x+20$ (in degrees). What is the size of angle $DAB$?","o":["$96^\\circ$","$108^\\circ$","$84^\\circ$","$32^\\circ$"],"a":0,"x":"Opposite angles in a cyclic quadrilateral add to $180^\\circ$. So $5x+20=180$, giving $x=32$. Then angle $DAB=3\\times32=96^\\circ$. The trap is to stop at $x$ or give the wrong angle.","w":["","This comes from solving $5x=180$ and forgetting the $+20$ in the equation.","This is angle $BCD$ ($2\\times32+20$), not angle $DAB$ as the question asks.","This is the value of $x$; the question asks for the angle $3x$."]}
{"id":"gma-198","s":"maths","t":"G9-G10","lv":3,"q":"$TAS$ is a tangent to a circle at $A$. $B$ and $C$ are on the circle, with $C$ on the major arc $AB$. Angle $TAB=65^\\circ$ and angle $ABC=40^\\circ$. What is angle $BAC$?","o":["$65^\\circ$","$115^\\circ$","$105^\\circ$","$75^\\circ$"],"a":3,"x":"By the alternate segment theorem, angle $ACB=65^\\circ$. In triangle $ABC$, angle $BAC=180-65-40=75^\\circ$. The trap is stopping early or using the wrong angles.","w":["This is angle $ACB$ from the alternate segment theorem, not angle $BAC$.","This is $180-65$, which ignores the triangle $ABC$.","This is $65+40$; the triangle angle needs $180-65-40$ instead.",""]}
{"id":"gma-199","s":"maths","t":"G9-G10","lv":3,"q":"Tangents from $T$ touch a circle, centre $O$, at $A$ and $B$. Angle $ATB=50^\\circ$. Work out angle $OAB$.","o":["$65^\\circ$","$25^\\circ$","$50^\\circ$","$40^\\circ$"],"a":1,"x":"Angles $OAT$ and $OBT$ are $90^\\circ$, so angle $AOB=360-90-90-50=130^\\circ$. Triangle $OAB$ is isosceles, so angle $OAB=(180-130)\\div2=25^\\circ$.","w":["This is angle $TAB$ from isosceles triangle $TAB$, not angle $OAB$.","","This copies the given angle at $T$, which is not linked to angle $OAB$ like this.","This is $90-50$, which wrongly assumes angle $OAB$ and angle $ATB$ are linked directly."]}
{"id":"gma-200","s":"maths","t":"G11-G15","lv":1,"q":"How many edges does a triangular prism have?","o":["6","9","5","12"],"a":1,"x":"A triangular prism has 2 triangular ends with 3 edges each, plus 3 edges joining them: 3+3+3=9. Do not mix up edges, faces and vertices.","w":["6 is the number of vertices, not edges.","","5 is the number of faces, not edges.","12 is the number of edges of a cuboid, not a triangular prism."]}
{"id":"gma-201","s":"maths","t":"G11-G15","lv":1,"q":"A map has a scale of 1 : 50 000. Two villages are 4 cm apart on the map. What is the real distance between them?","o":["0.2 km","20 km","2 km","200 km"],"a":2,"x":"4 × 50 000 = 200 000 cm. Divide by 100 to get 2000 m, then by 1000 to get 2 km. The trap is slipping when changing cm to km.","w":["Divided by 10 times too much when converting from cm to km.","Divided by 10 times too little when converting from cm to km.","","Divided 200 000 by 1000 only, treating centimetres as metres, so 100 times too big."]}
{"id":"gma-202","s":"maths","t":"G11-G15","lv":2,"q":"$A$ is the point $(-3, 4)$ and $B$ is the point $(5, -2)$. What are the coordinates of the midpoint of $AB$?","o":["$(2, 2)$","$(4, -3)$","$(1, 3)$","$(1, 1)$"],"a":3,"x":"Add the coordinates and halve: $(-3+5)/2=1$ and $(4+(-2))/2=1$. The midpoint is $(1,1)$. Take care with the negative numbers.","w":["Added the coordinates but forgot to halve them.","Halved the differences, which gives half the step from A to B, not the midpoint.","Made a sign slip on the y-values, using $4-(-2)$ instead of $4+(-2)$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-203","s":"maths","t":"G11-G15","lv":2,"q":"The bearing of $B$ from $A$ is $070^\\circ$. The bearing of $C$ from $B$ is $190^\\circ$. Work out the size of angle $ABC$.","o":["$60^\\circ$","$120^\\circ$","$110^\\circ$","$260^\\circ$"],"a":0,"x":"The bearing of A from B is $70+180=250^\\circ$. Angle ABC is the difference between $250^\\circ$ and $190^\\circ$, which is $60^\\circ$. The trap is using the bearing of B from A at B.","w":["","Did $190-70$, using the bearing of B from A instead of the back bearing at B.","Did $180-70$, which is not the angle between the two lines at B.","Added $70+190$ instead of using the back bearing and subtracting."]}
{"id":"gma-204","s":"maths","t":"G11-G15","lv":3,"q":"Sam walks 3 km due east, then 4 km due south. What is Sam's bearing from the start, to the nearest degree?","o":["$053^\\circ$","$127^\\circ$","$143^\\circ$","$233^\\circ$"],"a":2,"x":"Angle below east is $\\tan^{-1}(4/3)=53.1^\\circ$. Bearing is $90+53.1=143.1^\\circ$, so $143^\\circ$. The trap is giving the triangle angle, or using the wrong angle.","w":["This is only the angle inside the triangle, not measured clockwise from north.","Used $\\tan^{-1}(3/4)=36.9^\\circ$ then added it to $90^\\circ$, using the wrong angle.","","Added $180^\\circ$ to the triangle angle, which points south-west, not south-east."]}
{"id":"gma-205","s":"maths","t":"G16-G18","lv":1,"q":"A sector has radius 9 cm and angle $40^\\circ$. What is the length of its arc, in terms of $\\pi$?","o":["$\\pi$ cm","$2\\pi$ cm","$9\\pi$ cm","$18\\pi$ cm"],"a":1,"x":"Arc length $=\\frac{40}{360}\\times2\\pi\\times9=\\frac{1}{9}\\times18\\pi=2\\pi$. The trap is using $\\pi r$ or $\\pi r^2$ instead of $2\\pi r$.","w":["Used $\\pi r$ instead of $2\\pi r$ for the circumference, so the answer is half the correct size.","","That is the sector area ($\\frac{1}{9}\\times81\\pi$), not the arc length.","That is the whole circumference; forgot to multiply by $\\frac{40}{360}$."]}
{"id":"gma-206","s":"maths","t":"G16-G18","lv":1,"q":"A pyramid has a square base of side 6 cm and a vertical height of 5 cm. What is its volume?","o":["180 cm³","10 cm³","60 cm³","90 cm³"],"a":2,"x":"Volume $=\\frac{1}{3}\\times\\text{base area}\\times\\text{height}=\\frac{1}{3}\\times36\\times5=60$. The base area is $6\\times6$, not 6.","w":["Forgot the $\\frac{1}{3}$ and just multiplied base area by height.","Used the base length 6 instead of the base area 36.","","Used $\\frac{1}{2}$ instead of $\\frac{1}{3}$, as if the pyramid were half a prism."]}
{"id":"gma-207","s":"maths","t":"G16-G18","lv":2,"q":"A solid hemisphere has radius 3 cm. Its flat face is a circle. Sphere surface area is $4\\pi r^2$. What is the total surface area of the hemisphere, in terms of $\\pi$?","o":["$18\\pi$ cm²","$36\\pi$ cm²","$45\\pi$ cm²","$27\\pi$ cm²"],"a":3,"x":"Curved part $=\\frac{1}{2}\\times4\\pi\\times3^2=18\\pi$. Flat circle $=\\pi\\times3^2=9\\pi$. Total $=27\\pi$. The trap is forgetting the flat face.","w":["That is the curved surface only; left out the flat circular face.","That is the area of a whole sphere; forgot to halve it.","Added the flat face $9\\pi$ to a whole sphere's $36\\pi$; forgot to halve the curved part.",""]}
{"id":"gma-208","s":"maths","t":"G16-G18","lv":3,"q":"A solid metal sphere of radius 6 cm is melted and recast into a cylinder of radius 4 cm. No metal is lost. What is the height of the cylinder? Sphere volume is $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$.","o":["18 cm","9 cm","13.5 cm","36 cm"],"a":0,"x":"Sphere volume $=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi\\times216=288\\pi$. Cylinder volume $=\\pi\\times16\\times h$. So $16h=288$ and $h=18$. Volumes must be equal, not surface areas.","w":["","Used the sphere's surface area $144\\pi$ instead of its volume, then divided by $16\\pi$.","Forgot the $\\frac{4}{3}$ in the sphere volume, using $216\\pi\\div16\\pi$.","Used $2\\pi r=8\\pi$ for the cylinder's base area instead of $\\pi r^2=16\\pi$."]}
{"id":"gma-209","s":"maths","t":"G19-G21","lv":1,"q":"What is the exact value of $\\cos 30^\\circ$?","o":["$\\frac{1}{2}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}$","$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}$"],"a":1,"x":"$\\cos 30^\\circ=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$. A common trap is swapping it with $\\sin 30^\\circ=\\frac{1}{2}$. Learn the pair: $\\cos 30^\\circ$ is big, $\\cos 60^\\circ$ is small.","w":["This is $\\sin 30^\\circ$ (or $\\cos 60^\\circ$). Sin and cos values have been swapped.","","This is the exact value for $45^\\circ$, not $30^\\circ$.","This is $\\tan 30^\\circ$ written with a rational denominator, not $\\cos 30^\\circ$."]}
{"id":"gma-210","s":"maths","t":"G19-G21","lv":1,"q":"Two triangles are similar. The smaller has sides 4 cm, 6 cm and 8 cm. The shortest side of the larger is 10 cm. How long is its longest side?","o":["14 cm","16 cm","20 cm","25 cm"],"a":2,"x":"Scale factor $=10\\div4=2.5$. Longest side $=8\\times2.5=20$ cm. The trap is adding the difference (+6) instead of multiplying by the scale factor.","w":["Added 6 (the difference in shortest sides) to 8. Similar shapes use multiplying, not adding.","Doubled 8. The scale factor is 2.5, not 2.","","Multiplied 10 by 2.5. The scale factor should be applied to the 8 cm side."]}
{"id":"gma-211","s":"maths","t":"G19-G21","lv":2,"q":"A 5.0 m ladder leans against a vertical wall. Its foot is 1.4 m from the wall on level ground. How high up the wall does it reach? Give your answer to 1 decimal place.","o":["4.8 m","5.2 m","3.6 m","4.9 m"],"a":0,"x":"The ladder is the hypotenuse. $h^2=5^2-1.4^2=25-1.96=23.04$, so $h=4.8$ m. The trap is adding the squares instead of subtracting.","w":["","Added the squares: $\\sqrt{25+1.96}$. The ladder is the hypotenuse, so subtract.","Did $5-1.4$ without squaring. Pythagoras needs squares before subtracting.","Squared only the 5 and subtracted 1.4: $\\sqrt{25-1.4}$. Both sides must be squared."]}
{"id":"gma-212","s":"maths","t":"G19-G21","lv":3,"q":"Two mathematically similar cones have heights in the ratio 2 : 3. The smaller cone has volume 40 cm³. What is the volume of the larger cone?","o":["120 cm³","60 cm³","90 cm³","135 cm³"],"a":3,"x":"Length scale factor is $\\frac{3}{2}$. Volume scale factor is $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^3=\\frac{27}{8}$. $40\\times\\frac{27}{8}=135$ cm³. The trap is using the length ratio or the area ratio.","w":["Multiplied by 3 only. The ratio 2 : 3 gives a scale factor of 1.5, which must be cubed.","Multiplied by the length scale factor 1.5. Volume needs the cube of it.","Used the area scale factor $\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)^2$. Volume needs the cube.",""]}
{"id":"gma-213","s":"maths","t":"G19-G21","lv":3,"q":"An isosceles triangle has two equal sides of 10 cm and a base of 12 cm. Work out the size of one base angle, to 1 decimal place.","o":["$36.9^\\circ$","$59.0^\\circ$","$53.1^\\circ$","$73.7^\\circ$"],"a":2,"x":"Split the triangle into two right-angled triangles. Half the base is 6 cm, hypotenuse 10 cm. $\\cos x=\\frac{6}{10}$, so $x=53.1^\\circ$. The trap is mixing up sin and cos.","w":["Used $\\sin^{-1}(0.6)$, which gives the angle at the top of the right-angled triangle, not the base angle.","Used $\\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{10}{6}\\right)$, treating 10 as the opposite side. It is the hypotenuse.","","This is the angle at the top (apex) of the isosceles triangle, not a base angle."]}
{"id":"gma-214","s":"maths","t":"G22-G23","lv":3,"q":"Triangle ABC has AB = 7 cm, AC = 8 cm and BC = 9 cm. Work out the size of angle $A$, to 1 decimal place.","o":["$48.2^\\circ$","$16.6^\\circ$","$73.4^\\circ$","$55.2^\\circ$"],"a":2,"x":"Angle $A$ is opposite the longest side, 9. $\\cos A=\\frac{7^2+8^2-9^2}{2\\times7\\times8}=\\frac{32}{112}$, so $A=73.4^\\circ$. The trap is using the wrong side as the opposite side, or dropping the 2.","w":["Found the angle opposite the side of length 7, not the side of length 9.","Used $\\sin^{-1}$ on $\\frac{32}{112}$ instead of $\\cos^{-1}$.","","Left out the 2 in $2bc$ and divided by $7\\times8=56$ only."]}
{"id":"gma-215","s":"maths","t":"G22-G23","lv":3,"q":"In triangle ABC, AB = 10 cm, angle $A=40^\\circ$ and angle $B=65^\\circ$. Work out the area of the triangle, to 3 significant figures.","o":["$68.1\\text{ cm}^2$","$30.2\\text{ cm}^2$","$60.3\\text{ cm}^2$","$21.4\\text{ cm}^2$"],"a":1,"x":"Angle $C=75^\\circ$. Sine rule: $BC=\\frac{10\\sin40^\\circ}{\\sin75^\\circ}=6.655$. Area $=\\frac{1}{2}\\times10\\times6.655\\times\\sin65^\\circ=30.2$. The trap is mixing up which side goes with which angle.","w":["Swapped the sine rule ratio, using $\\frac{10\\sin75^\\circ}{\\sin40^\\circ}$ for BC.","","Forgot the $\\frac{1}{2}$ in the area formula, doubling the answer.","Used $\\sin40^\\circ$ in the area, but the angle between AB and BC is $65^\\circ$."]}
{"id":"gma-216","s":"maths","t":"G24-G25","lv":1,"q":"Point A is at (2, 1). It is translated by the vector $\\begin{pmatrix}-3\\\\5\\end{pmatrix}$. What are the coordinates of the new point?","o":["(-1, -4)","(5, -4)","(7, -2)","(-1, 6)"],"a":3,"x":"The top number moves the point left or right, and the bottom number moves it up or down. So $(2-3,\\ 1+5)=(-1,6)$. The trap is subtracting the vector or mixing up the signs.","w":["Subtracted 5 from the y-coordinate instead of adding it, a sign slip on the bottom number.","Subtracted the whole vector instead of adding it, which moves the point the wrong way.","Added the numbers the wrong way round, using 5 for x and -3 for y.",""]}
{"id":"gma-217","s":"maths","t":"G24-G25","lv":1,"q":"$\\overrightarrow{AB}=2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}$. Which expression is $\\overrightarrow{BA}$?","o":["$\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}$","$2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}$","$2\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b}$","$-2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b}$"],"a":0,"x":"Going from B to A is the reverse of going from A to B. So $\\overrightarrow{BA}=-\\overrightarrow{AB}$. Change the sign of every term: $-(2\\mathbf{a}-\\mathbf{b})=\\mathbf{b}-2\\mathbf{a}$.","w":["","This is the same as $\\overrightarrow{AB}$. The direction was not reversed.","Changed only the sign of the $\\mathbf{b}$ term, not both terms.","Changed only the sign of the $\\mathbf{a}$ term, not both terms."]}
{"id":"gma-218","s":"maths","t":"G24-G25","lv":2,"q":"Which of these vectors is parallel to $\\begin{pmatrix}6\\\\-9\\end{pmatrix}$?","o":["$\\begin{pmatrix}4\\\\6\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}9\\\\-6\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-4\\\\6\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\-6\\end{pmatrix}$"],"a":2,"x":"Parallel vectors are multiples of each other. Multiplying $\\begin{pmatrix}6\\\\-9\\end{pmatrix}$ by $-\\tfrac{2}{3}$ gives $\\begin{pmatrix}-4\\\\6\\end{pmatrix}$. A negative multiple is still parallel.","w":["The size matches but one sign is wrong. Both numbers must be scaled by the same multiple.","Swapped the top and bottom numbers. That changes the direction.","","Halved the top number but not the bottom. The same multiple must be applied to both."]}
{"id":"gma-219","s":"maths","t":"G24-G25","lv":2,"q":"Translation $\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\begin{pmatrix}3\\\\2\\end{pmatrix}$ and translation $\\overrightarrow{BC}=\\begin{pmatrix}-5\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$. Which vector is $\\overrightarrow{CA}$?","o":["$\\begin{pmatrix}-2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}8\\\\1\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\-3\\end{pmatrix}$","$\\begin{pmatrix}-8\\\\-1\\end{pmatrix}$"],"a":2,"x":"$\\overrightarrow{AC}=\\overrightarrow{AB}+\\overrightarrow{BC}=\\begin{pmatrix}-2\\\\3\\end{pmatrix}$. But $\\overrightarrow{CA}$ goes the other way, so reverse the signs: $\\begin{pmatrix}2\\\\-3\\end{pmatrix}$.","w":["This is $\\overrightarrow{AC}$. It answers A to C, not C to A.","Subtracted $\\overrightarrow{BC}$ from $\\overrightarrow{AB}$ instead of adding.","","Subtracted instead of adding, then reversed the direction."]}
{"id":"gma-220","s":"maths","t":"G24-G25","lv":3,"q":"$\\overrightarrow{OA}=\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\overrightarrow{OB}=\\mathbf{b}$. Point C is on AB so that $AC:CB=1:2$. Find $\\overrightarrow{OC}$.","o":["$\\tfrac{1}{3}(\\mathbf{a}+2\\mathbf{b})$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}(2\\mathbf{a}+\\mathbf{b})$","$\\mathbf{a}+\\tfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{b}$","$\\tfrac{1}{3}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})$"],"a":1,"x":"$\\overrightarrow{AB}=\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}$. AC is $\\tfrac{1}{3}$ of AB. So $\\overrightarrow{OC}=\\mathbf{a}+\\tfrac{1}{3}(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a})=\\tfrac{2}{3}\\mathbf{a}+\\tfrac{1}{3}\\mathbf{b}$. The trap is using $\\mathbf{b}$ instead of $\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}$.","w":["Used the ratio the wrong way round, which puts C two thirds of the way along AB.","","Took $\\tfrac{1}{3}$ of $\\mathbf{b}$ instead of $\\tfrac{1}{3}$ of $\\overrightarrow{AB}$, so $\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}$ was missed.","This is only $\\overrightarrow{AC}$. It forgot to start from O by adding $\\overrightarrow{OA}$."]}
{"id":"gma-221","s":"maths","t":"P1-P5","lv":1,"q":"Which of these can NOT be the probability of an event?","o":["$0.9$","$\\frac{1}{8}$","$125\\%$","$0$"],"a":2,"x":"A probability must be between 0 and 1 (0% to 100%). $125\\%$ is more than 1, so it is impossible. $0$ is a valid probability: it means the event cannot happen.","w":["$0.9$ is between 0 and 1, so it is a valid probability (a very likely event).","$\\frac{1}{8}$ is between 0 and 1, so it is valid. Fractions can be probabilities.","","$0$ is valid. It means impossible. The slip is thinking probabilities must be above 0."]}
{"id":"gma-222","s":"maths","t":"P1-P5","lv":1,"q":"A spinner is spun 50 times. It lands on red 18 times. What is the relative frequency of red?","o":["$0.36$","$0.18$","$0.64$","$\\frac{18}{32}$"],"a":0,"x":"Relative frequency = number of times it happened ÷ total number of trials. $18\\div 50=0.36$. The trap is dividing by the 'not red' count instead of the total.","w":["","Divided 18 by 100 instead of by the 50 spins.","This is the relative frequency of NOT red ($32\\div50$), not of red.","Compared red with not-red (18 to 32) instead of dividing by the total of 50."]}
{"id":"gma-223","s":"maths","t":"P1-P5","lv":2,"q":"A biased dice has $P(1)=0.1$, $P(2)=0.15$, $P(3)=0.2$ and $P(4)=0.25$. $P(5)$ and $P(6)$ are equal. What is $P(5)$?","o":["$0.3$","$0.7$","$0.05$","$0.15$"],"a":3,"x":"All the probabilities add to 1. The first four add to $0.7$, so $P(5)+P(6)=0.3$. They are equal, so $P(5)=0.3\\div2=0.15$. The trap is forgetting to share.","w":["This is $P(5)+P(6)$ together. Forgot to split it between the two equal outcomes.","This is the total of the first four probabilities, not the missing one.","Divided $0.3$ by 6 (the number of faces) instead of by 2 (the two equal outcomes).",""]}
{"id":"gma-224","s":"maths","t":"P1-P5","lv":2,"q":"Amy spins a spinner 40 times and gets a 3 on 14 spins. Raj spins it 60 times and gets a 3 on 27 spins. What is the best estimate of the probability of a 3?","o":["$0.40$","$0.41$","$0.45$","$0.35$"],"a":1,"x":"Combine all the results: $(14+27)\\div(40+60)=41\\div100=0.41$. More trials give a better estimate. The trap is averaging the two relative frequencies ($0.35$ and $0.45$), which ignores the different numbers of spins.","w":["Averaged $0.35$ and $0.45$. This ignores that Raj spun more times.","","Used only Raj's result. The best estimate uses all the trials together.","Used only Amy's result. The best estimate uses all the trials together."]}
{"id":"gma-225","s":"maths","t":"P1-P5","lv":3,"q":"A bag has red, yellow and blue counters. $P(\\text{blue})=0.2$. $P(\\text{red})$ is three times $P(\\text{yellow})$. What is $P(\\text{red})$?","o":["$0.6$","$0.27$","$0.8$","$0.2$"],"a":0,"x":"Red + yellow = $1-0.2=0.8$. Let yellow = $y$, red = $3y$, so $4y=0.8$ and $y=0.2$. Red = $0.6$. The trap is stopping before sharing $0.8$ in the ratio 3 : 1.","w":["","Divided $0.8$ by 3 instead of sharing it in the ratio 3 : 1.","This is red and yellow together. It was not shared between them.","This is $P(\\text{yellow})$, not $P(\\text{red})$. Red is three times as big."]}
{"id":"gma-226","s":"maths","t":"P6-P8","lv":1,"q":"Two fair six-sided dice are rolled and the scores are added. What is the probability that the total is 5?","o":["$\\frac{1}{11}$","$\\frac{1}{9}$","$\\frac{1}{18}$","$\\frac{5}{36}$"],"a":1,"x":"There are 36 equally likely outcomes. Total 5 comes from (1,4), (2,3), (3,2), (4,1), which is 4 outcomes. $\\frac{4}{36}=\\frac{1}{9}$. The trap is treating the totals as equally likely.","w":["Treated the 11 possible totals (2 to 12) as equally likely, but they are not.","","Counted only (1,4) and (2,3), missing (4,1) and (3,2); order matters on two dice.","Used the total 5 as the numerator instead of counting the outcomes that give 5."]}
{"id":"gma-227","s":"maths","t":"P6-P8","lv":2,"q":"40 students: 22 play football, 15 play hockey and 8 play both. One student is chosen at random. What is the probability that they play neither sport?","o":["$\\frac{3}{40}$","$\\frac{29}{40}$","$\\frac{11}{40}$","$\\frac{21}{40}$"],"a":2,"x":"In the Venn diagram, football or hockey is $22+15-8=29$. Neither is $40-29=11$. So the probability is $\\frac{11}{40}$. The trap is counting the overlap twice.","w":["Worked out $40-22-15$, which counts the 8 who play both twice.","That is P(plays at least one sport), not P(neither).","","That is P(exactly one sport), which is $14+7=21$ students, not neither."]}
{"id":"gma-228","s":"maths","t":"P6-P8","lv":2,"q":"A bag has 5 red and 3 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random without replacement. What is the probability that they are different colours?","o":["$\\frac{15}{28}$","$\\frac{15}{56}$","$\\frac{15}{32}$","$\\frac{13}{28}$"],"a":0,"x":"Red then blue: $\\frac{5}{8}\\times\\frac{3}{7}=\\frac{15}{56}$. Blue then red is also $\\frac{15}{56}$. Add both orders: $\\frac{30}{56}=\\frac{15}{28}$. The trap is using only one order.","w":["","Found only one order (red then blue). Different colours can happen in two orders.","Used 8 on the bottom both times, as if the first counter was replaced.","That is P(same colour): $\\frac{20}{56}+\\frac{6}{56}$. It is the wrong event."]}
{"id":"gma-229","s":"maths","t":"P6-P8","lv":2,"q":"A train is on time with probability 0.9 each day. Days are independent. What is the probability that it is late at least once in 3 days?","o":["$0.3$","$0.729$","$0.001$","$0.271$"],"a":3,"x":"P(on time all 3 days) $=0.9^3=0.729$. P(late at least once) $=1-0.729=0.271$. The trap is stopping at 0.729, which is the opposite event.","w":["Added $0.1+0.1+0.1$. Adding ignores that days can both be late, so it overcounts.","That is P(on time all three days), the opposite of the event asked for.","That is P(late all three days) $=0.1^3$, not late at least once.",""]}
{"id":"gma-230","s":"maths","t":"P6-P8","lv":3,"q":"A bag has 4 mint and 6 toffee sweets. Three sweets are taken at random without replacement. What is the probability that at least one is mint?","o":["$\\frac{1}{6}$","$\\frac{1}{30}$","$\\frac{5}{6}$","$\\frac{98}{125}$"],"a":2,"x":"P(no mint) $=\\frac{6}{10}\\times\\frac{5}{9}\\times\\frac{4}{8}=\\frac{1}{6}$. So P(at least one mint) $=1-\\frac{1}{6}=\\frac{5}{6}$. The numbers drop each time because there is no replacement.","w":["That is P(no mint). It is the opposite of the event asked for.","That is P(all three mint) $=\\frac{4}{10}\\times\\frac{3}{9}\\times\\frac{2}{8}$, not at least one.","","Used $1-0.6^3$, which keeps 10 sweets each time. That is with replacement, not without."]}
{"id":"gma-231","s":"maths","t":"P9","lv":3,"q":"A bag has 5 red and 3 blue counters. Two counters are taken at random without replacement. Given that both are the same colour, what is the probability that both are red?","o":["$\\frac{5}{14}$","$\\frac{13}{28}$","$\\frac{10}{13}$","$\\frac{25}{34}$"],"a":2,"x":"P(RR) $=\\frac{5}{8}\\times\\frac{4}{7}=\\frac{20}{56}$. P(BB) $=\\frac{3}{8}\\times\\frac{2}{7}=\\frac{6}{56}$. Same colour is $\\frac{26}{56}$. So the answer is $\\frac{20}{26}=\\frac{10}{13}$. The trap is not restricting to same-colour outcomes.","w":["This is P(both red) with no condition; it ignores that we are told the counters match.","This is P(same colour), not P(both red given same colour).","","Used replacement, so the probabilities stayed 5/8 and 3/8; the counters are not put back."]}
{"id":"gma-232","s":"maths","t":"P9","lv":3,"q":"40 students: 22 study French, 18 study Spanish and 8 study neither. A student who studies French is chosen at random. What is the probability they also study Spanish?","o":["$\\frac{1}{5}$","$\\frac{4}{9}$","$\\frac{1}{4}$","$\\frac{4}{11}$"],"a":3,"x":"Let $x$ study both: $22+18-x+8=40$, so $x=8$. We are told the student studies French, so the total is 22. The probability is $\\frac{8}{22}=\\frac{4}{11}$. The trap is using the wrong group as the denominator.","w":["This is 8 out of all 40 students; the condition 'studies French' was ignored.","This divides by the 18 Spanish students; the given group is French students.","This divides by 32, the students studying at least one language; the group should be French students only.",""]}
{"id":"gma-233","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":1,"q":"A head teacher wants a fair sample of the views of all 900 pupils on school meals. Which sample is most likely to be unbiased?","o":["The first 30 pupils in the lunch queue","30 pupils chosen using random numbers from the full school list","The 30 members of the school football team","30 pupils from Year 7 only"],"a":1,"x":"A random sample from the full list gives every pupil an equal chance of being picked. The other samples leave out some groups, so they are biased.","w":["The queue only has pupils who buy lunch, who may like the meals more; it is not random.","","The football team is just one group of pupils, and its views may not match the rest of the school.","Using one year group ignores the other years, so the sample does not represent the whole school."]}
{"id":"gma-234","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":1,"q":"45 people were asked how they travel to work. 18 said cycling. In a pie chart, what angle should the cycling sector have?","o":["$40^\\circ$","$18^\\circ$","$20^\\circ$","$144^\\circ$"],"a":3,"x":"The angle is $\\frac{18}{45}\\times360=144^\\circ$. The trap is using the frequency or the percentage as the angle instead of a fraction of $360^\\circ$.","w":["40 is the percentage who cycle; you need 40% of $360^\\circ$, not 40 itself.","Used the frequency 18 as the angle; the angle is the fraction $\\frac{18}{45}$ of $360^\\circ$.","Did $360\\div18$; each person is $360\\div45=8^\\circ$, so 18 people give $144^\\circ$.",""]}
{"id":"gma-235","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":2,"q":"80 students are asked how they get to school. 45 are girls. 30 students walk in total. 12 of the boys walk. How many girls walk?","o":["$18$","$33$","$23$","$12$"],"a":0,"x":"Boys = 80 − 45 = 35. Girls who walk = 30 − 12 = 18. The trap is subtracting the wrong totals, such as the number of girls, instead of using the total who walk.","w":["","Did 45 − 12, taking the boys who walk away from all the girls, which mixes up the groups.","Did 35 − 12, which gives the boys who do not walk, not the girls who walk.","12 is the number of boys who walk; the girls who walk are the total who walk minus these."]}
{"id":"gma-236","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":2,"q":"A time-series graph shows monthly ice cream sales for three years. Sales peak every July. Each July peak is higher than the one before. Which statement is correct?","o":["There is no pattern, so sales change at random","Sales are falling steadily over the three years","There is a yearly seasonal pattern and sales are rising overall","There is a seasonal pattern and sales are falling overall"],"a":2,"x":"The peaks repeating each July show seasonal variation. Higher peaks each year show an upward trend. The trap is confusing the repeating pattern with the overall trend.","w":["The peaks repeat every July, so there is a clear pattern and it is not random.","The July peaks get higher, so the overall trend is upward, not downward.","","The seasonal pattern is right, but the peaks rise each year, so the overall trend is upward not falling."]}
{"id":"gma-237","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":3,"q":"In a pie chart of favourite sports, the football sector is $120^\\circ$ and stands for 18 pupils. The tennis sector is $80^\\circ$. How many pupils chose tennis?","o":["$27$","$12$","$80$","$8$"],"a":1,"x":"Each $120^\\circ$ is 18 pupils, so $1^\\circ$ is $0.15$ pupils. Then $80\\times0.15=12$. The trap is putting the ratio the wrong way round.","w":["Worked out $18\\times\\frac{120}{80}$, which turns the ratio upside down; a smaller angle must give fewer pupils.","","Used the angle 80 as the number of pupils; the angle must be scaled using 18 pupils per $120^\\circ$.","Divided the angle by 10, which has no link to the scale given in the question."]}
{"id":"gma-238","s":"maths","t":"S3","lv":3,"q":"A histogram uses frequency density. The bar for $20 \\le x < 60$ has height 1.2. Assuming the values are spread evenly, estimate how many values in this class are less than 35.","o":["$48$","$15$","$42$","$18$"],"a":3,"x":"Frequency $=$ frequency density $\\times$ width. The part from 20 to 35 has width 15, so $15\\times1.2=18$. The trap is using the whole class or the wrong width.","w":["This is the frequency of the whole class ($40\\times1.2$), not just the part below 35.","Used the width 15 as the answer and forgot to multiply by the frequency density 1.2.","Used $35\\times1.2$; the part below 35 in this class starts at 20, so its width is 15.",""]}
{"id":"gma-240","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":1,"q":"Class A test marks: mean 62, range 10. Class B test marks: mean 58, range 25. Which comparison is correct?","o":["Class B did better on average and its marks were more consistent","Class B had the bigger range, so its marks were closer together","Class A did better on average and its marks were more consistent","Class A did better on average but its marks were more spread out"],"a":2,"x":"A higher mean means better on average. A smaller range means more consistent marks. Class A has the higher mean (62) and the smaller range (10). The trap is thinking a bigger range means more consistent.","w":["Class B has the lower mean and the bigger range, so it did worse on average and was less consistent.","A bigger range means the marks are more spread out, not closer together.","","Class A's range is smaller, 10 against 25, so its marks are less spread out, not more."]}
{"id":"gma-241","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":1,"q":"A box plot for some times shows: minimum 5, lower quartile 12, median 20, upper quartile 31, maximum 44. What is the interquartile range?","o":["$19$","$39$","$11$","$26$"],"a":0,"x":"The interquartile range is upper quartile minus lower quartile: $31-12=19$. The trap is using the minimum and maximum, which gives the range, not the IQR.","w":["","This is the range, $44-5$, but the IQR uses the quartiles, not the minimum and maximum.","This is $31-20$, upper quartile minus median, but the IQR is upper quartile minus lower quartile.","This is $31-5$, upper quartile minus minimum, but the IQR subtracts the lower quartile, $12$."]}
{"id":"gma-242","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":2,"q":"Boys' 100 m times: median 14.2 s, IQR 0.8 s. Girls' 100 m times: median 15.1 s, IQR 1.6 s. Which statement is correct?","o":["Girls were typically faster because their median is higher","Boys were typically faster, but their times were less consistent","Girls' times were more consistent because their IQR is bigger","Boys were typically faster and their times were more consistent"],"a":3,"x":"For times, a lower median means faster. Boys' median is lower (14.2 s). A smaller IQR means more consistent, and the boys' IQR is 0.8 s. The trap is treating a higher median as better for times.","w":["A higher time means slower, so the girls' higher median shows they were typically slower.","The boys' IQR, 0.8 s, is smaller than the girls' 1.6 s, so boys were more consistent.","A bigger IQR means more spread, so the girls' times were less consistent, not more.",""]}
{"id":"gma-243","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":2,"q":"Five values are: 4, 5, 5, 6, 90. Which average best shows the typical value, and why?","o":["The mean, 22, because it uses every value","The median, 5, because the 90 does not affect it","The range, 86, because it shows the whole data set","The mean, 22, because it is not affected by the extreme value"],"a":1,"x":"The extreme value 90 pulls the mean up to $110\\div5=22$, far from most values. The median, 5, is not affected by it. The trap is choosing the mean because it uses every value.","w":["The mean does use every value, but the extreme $90$ drags it up to $22$, far from most values.","","The range measures spread, not an average, so it cannot show a typical value.","The mean is affected by extreme values; here $90$ pulls it up to $22$, so this reason is false."]}
{"id":"gma-244","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":3,"q":"8 boys have a mean mark of 55. 12 girls have a mean mark of 65. What is the mean mark of all 20 students together?","o":["$60$","$59$","$61$","$65$"],"a":2,"x":"Boys' total $=8\\times55=440$. Girls' total $=12\\times65=780$. All together $=1220$, and $1220\\div20=61$. The trap is averaging the two means, which ignores the different group sizes.","w":["This is $(55+65)\\div2$, which ignores that there are more girls, so the mean must be nearer 65.","This swaps the group sizes, $(12\\times55+8\\times65)\\div20=59$; the girls' mean should be weighted by 12.","","This is only the girls' mean; the 8 boys score lower, so the overall mean must be below $65$."]}
{"id":"gma-245","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":1,"q":"Which statement is true for a good line of best fit drawn by eye on a scatter graph?","o":["It must go through the first and last points.","It must always go through the origin.","It follows the trend, with about as many points above it as below it.","It must join up every point on the graph."],"a":2,"x":"A line of best fit shows the trend. It should have roughly equal numbers of points on each side. It does not have to go through any particular point, including the origin.","w":["The line need not touch the first or last point, because it fits the trend of all the points.","Many lines of best fit miss the origin, so assuming they must pass through it is a common slip.","","A line of best fit is one straight line showing the trend, so it cannot join every point."]}
{"id":"gma-246","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":1,"q":"A scatter graph shows age (5 to 15 years) and height for some children. A line of best fit is used to predict the height of a 40-year-old. Why is this unreliable?","o":["40 is outside the range of the data, so the trend may not continue.","A line of best fit can only predict for even ages.","The correlation between age and height is negative.","Predictions are only reliable if the line goes through the origin."],"a":0,"x":"Predicting outside the range of the data is extrapolation. The trend may not continue, because people stop growing. Predicting inside the range (interpolation) is more reliable.","w":["","There is no such rule, because a line of best fit can be used for any value within the data range.","Height tends to rise with age, so the correlation is positive, not negative.","The line need not pass through the origin, and the real problem is that 40 is outside the data."]}
{"id":"gma-247","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":2,"q":"A scatter graph shows the age and value of used cars. The line of best fit passes through (2 years, £12 000) and (8 years, £6000). Estimate the value of a 5-year-old car.","o":["£7000","£10 000","£8000","£9000"],"a":3,"x":"The value falls by £6000 over 6 years, which is £1000 a year. From 2 years to 5 years is 3 years, so take away £3000 from £12 000 to get £9000. The trap is counting the years wrongly.","w":["This takes away 5 lots of £1000, forgetting that the line starts at 2 years, so it gives the 7-year value.","This takes away only 2 lots of £1000, which gives the value at 4 years, not 5.","This takes away 4 lots of £1000, which gives the value at 6 years, not 5.",""]}
{"id":"gma-248","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":2,"q":"A scatter graph shows strong positive correlation between daily ice cream sales and daily sunburn cases at a beach. Which conclusion is best?","o":["Eating ice cream causes sunburn.","Hot, sunny weather probably affects both, so one does not necessarily cause the other.","Sunburn causes people to buy ice cream.","There is no relationship between the two."],"a":1,"x":"Correlation does not prove one thing causes the other. A third factor, hot sunny weather, probably increases both. The trap is assuming that correlation means cause.","w":["This treats correlation as proof of cause, when the two are really linked through the weather.","","This also assumes cause from correlation, when the weather is the likely reason both rise.","Strong positive correlation means there is a clear relationship, even if neither thing causes the other."]}
{"id":"gma-249","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":3,"q":"A scatter graph has data for x from 5 to 35. The line of best fit passes through (10, 24) and (30, 48). Use the line to estimate y when x = 25.","o":["36","40","42","54"],"a":2,"x":"The gradient is (48 − 24) ÷ (30 − 10) = 1.2. From x = 10 to x = 25 is 15, so y = 24 + 15 × 1.2 = 42. The trap is treating y as directly proportional to x.","w":["This is the value at x = 20, the midpoint of the two given points, not at x = 25.","This takes 25/30 of 48, wrongly assuming the line passes through the origin, which it does not.","","This works out 24 + 25 × 1.2, adding the change from x = 0 instead of from x = 10."]}
{"id":"gma-fig-010","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows revision time and test score for some students, with a line of best fit. What does the gradient of the line of best fit tell you?","o":["Each extra hour of revision adds about 10 marks","Each extra hour of revision adds about 5 marks","Each extra hour of revision adds about 0.2 marks","Each extra hour of revision adds about 30 marks"],"a":1,"x":"Pick two points on the line: (2, 20) and (8, 50). The gradient is (50 − 20) ÷ (8 − 2) = 30 ÷ 6 = 5. So each extra hour of revision adds about 5 marks.","w":["Reads the intercept (the score at 0 hours) as if it were the gradient.","","Divides change in hours by change in marks. This turns the gradient upside down.","Uses only the rise in marks and forgets to divide by the change in hours."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,60],"xstep":2,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"revision time / hours","ylabel":"test score / marks","lines":[{"pts":[[1,14],[2,22],[3,23],[4,32],[5,34],[6,38],[7,47],[8,48],[9,57],[10,56]],"dots":true,"c":0},{"pts":[[0,10],[10,60]],"c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-011","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the age and value of some cars, with a line of best fit. Car P does not fit the trend. How much more is P worth than the line of best fit suggests for a car of its age?","o":["£16 000","£4000","£24 000","£8000"],"a":3,"x":"P is at age 6 years and £16 000. The line of best fit at 6 years is £8000. The difference is 16 − 8 = 8, so P is worth £8000 more than the line suggests.","w":["Gives the value of P itself and forgets to take away the line's estimate.","Compares P with the line at age 4 years instead of at age 6 years.","Adds P's value and the line's value instead of finding the difference.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,10],"y":[0,20],"xstep":2,"ystep":4,"xlabel":"age / years","ylabel":"value / £1000","lines":[{"pts":[[1,19],[2,15],[3,15],[4,11],[5,9],[7,7],[8,4],[9,3]],"dots":true,"c":0},{"pts":[[0,20],[10,0]],"c":1}],"points":[{"at":[6,16],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-007","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows a cycle ride from home. What was the cyclist's speed in the last part of the ride?","o":["30 km/h","20 km/h","10 km/h","0.1 km/h"],"a":2,"x":"In the last part the distance goes from 40 km to 10 km. That is a change of 30 km. It takes 3 hours (from 3 h to 6 h). Speed = 30 ÷ 3 = 10 km/h, heading back towards home.","w":["Used the change in distance only and did not divide by the time taken.","Used the speed of the first part of the ride, which is steeper, instead of the last part.","","Divided time by distance instead of distance by time, so the gradient is upside down."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,80],"xstep":1,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"time / hours","ylabel":"distance from home / km","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[2,40],[3,40],[6,10]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-008","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows the speed of a train as it travels along a straight track. Work out the total distance the train travels in the time shown.","o":["102 m","90 m","120 m","140 m"],"a":0,"x":"Distance is the area under the graph. Trapezium 0 to 6 s: ½ × (4 + 10) × 6 = 42. Rectangle: 4 × 10 = 40. Triangle: ½ × 4 × 10 = 20. Total = 102 m.","w":["","Treated the first part as a triangle and ignored the starting speed of the train.","Used 6 × 10 for the first part, which is a rectangle at the top speed, not a trapezium.","Used one big rectangle: the top speed multiplied by the whole time."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,14],"y":[0,12],"xstep":2,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"speed / m/s","lines":[{"pts":[[0,4],[6,10],[10,10],[14,0]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-009","s":"maths","t":"A14-A15","lv":3,"q":"The curve shows how far a toy car has travelled. The dashed line is a tangent to the curve at P. Use the tangent to estimate the speed of the car at P.","o":["2 m/s","8 m/s","0.25 m/s","4 m/s"],"a":3,"x":"Speed is the gradient of the tangent at P. The tangent passes through (2, 0) and (6, 16). Gradient = change in distance ÷ change in time = 16 ÷ 4 = 4 m/s.","w":["Divided the distance at P by the time at P. That is the gradient from the origin, not the tangent.","Read the distance at P off the vertical axis instead of finding a gradient.","Worked out time ÷ distance, so the gradient is upside down.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,20],"xstep":1,"ystep":4,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"distance / m","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,0.0],[0.0375,0.000703],[0.075,0.002812],[0.1125,0.006328],[0.15,0.01125],[0.1875,0.017578],[0.225,0.025313],[0.2625,0.034453],[0.3,0.045],[0.3375,0.056953],[0.375,0.070312],[0.4125,0.085078],[0.45,0.10125],[0.4875,0.118828],[0.525,0.137813],[0.5625,0.158203],[0.6,0.18],[0.6375,0.203203],[0.675,0.227813],[0.7125,0.253828],[0.75,0.28125],[0.7875,0.310078],[0.825,0.340312],[0.8625,0.371953],[0.9,0.405],[0.9375,0.439453],[0.975,0.475312],[1.0125,0.512578],[1.05,0.55125],[1.0875,0.591328],[1.125,0.632812],[1.1625,0.675703],[1.2,0.72],[1.2375,0.765703],[1.275,0.812812],[1.3125,0.861328],[1.35,0.91125],[1.3875,0.962578],[1.425,1.015313],[1.4625,1.069453],[1.5,1.125],[1.5375,1.181953],[1.575,1.240312],[1.6125,1.300078],[1.65,1.36125],[1.6875,1.423828],[1.725,1.487813],[1.7625,1.553203],[1.8,1.62],[1.8375,1.688203],[1.875,1.757812],[1.9125,1.828828],[1.95,1.90125],[1.9875,1.975078],[2.025,2.050312],[2.0625,2.126953],[2.1,2.205],[2.1375,2.284453],[2.175,2.365312],[2.2125,2.447578],[2.25,2.53125],[2.2875,2.616328],[2.325,2.702813],[2.3625,2.790703],[2.4,2.88],[2.4375,2.970703],[2.475,3.062813],[2.5125,3.156328],[2.55,3.25125],[2.5875,3.347578],[2.625,3.445312],[2.6625,3.544453],[2.7,3.645],[2.7