{"id":"gma-fig-031","s":"maths","t":"A21-A22","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the cost of hiring a bike from shop A and from shop B for $h$ hours. Which inequality gives the times for which shop B is cheaper than shop A?","o":["$h>4$","$h<4$","$h>40$","$h<40$"],"a":1,"x":"The lines cross at 4 hours, where the cost is £40. Line B is below line A to the left of this point. So B is cheaper when $h<4$.","w":["Takes the wrong line as the cheaper one. B is lower before the crossing, not after it.","","Reads the cost (£40) at the crossing instead of the time, and picks the wrong side.","Reads the cost axis value instead of the time. The inequality is about hours, so use the horizontal axis."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,6],"y":[0,60],"xstep":1,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"time / hours","ylabel":"cost / £","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[6,50]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,8],[6,56]],"label":"B","c":1}],"points":[{"at":[4,40],"label":"P"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-032","s":"maths","t":"A21-A22","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the total cost of hiring a hall for $n$ people. Mr Khan has a budget of £100. Use the graph to find the largest number of people he can invite.","o":["20","25","15","75"],"a":2,"x":"The line starts at £25 (the fixed charge) and rises £25 every 5 people, so £5 per person. $25+5n\\le100$, so $5n\\le75$ and $n\\le15$. On the graph, £100 is at $n=15$.","w":["Divides £100 by £5 and ignores the £25 fixed charge shown where the line starts.","Adds the £25 fixed charge to £100 instead of taking it away, then divides by 5.","","Finds the money left after the fixed charge (£75) but forgets to divide by £5 per person."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,20],"y":[0,150],"xstep":5,"ystep":25,"xlabel":"number of people, n","ylabel":"total cost / £","lines":[{"pts":[[0,25],[20,125]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[0,25],"label":"P"},{"at":[20,125],"label":"Q"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-018","s":"maths","t":"A13","lv":3,"q":"P is the minimum point of the graph of $y=f(x)$. Use the graph to find the coordinates of the minimum point of the graph of $y=f(-x)+3$.","o":["$(2,-1)$","$(-2,-7)$","$(-2,-1)$","$(2,7)$"],"a":2,"x":"P is at $(2,-4)$. $f(-x)$ reflects the graph in the y-axis, so P goes to $(-2,-4)$. The $+3$ moves it up 3 to $(-2,-1)$.","w":["Moved the point up 3 but forgot the reflection in the y-axis, so the x-coordinate stayed as 2.","Reflected in the y-axis but then moved the point down 3 instead of up 3.","","Treated $f(-x)$ as $-f(x)$, which flips in the x-axis, so $(2,-4)$ became $(2,4)$, then added 3."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[-1,5],"y":[-6,6],"xstep":1,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"x","ylabel":"y","lines":[{"pts":[[-1.0,5.0],[-0.9625,4.776406],[-0.925,4.555625],[-0.8875,4.337656],[-0.85,4.1225],[-0.8125,3.910156],[-0.775,3.700625],[-0.7375,3.493906],[-0.7,3.29],[-0.6625,3.088906],[-0.625,2.890625],[-0.5875,2.695156],[-0.55,2.5025],[-0.5125,2.312656],[-0.475,2.125625],[-0.4375,1.941406],[-0.4,1.76],[-0.3625,1.581406],[-0.325,1.405625],[-0.2875,1.232656],[-0.25,1.0625],[-0.2125,0.895156],[-0.175,0.730625],[-0.1375,0.568906],[-0.1,0.41],[-0.0625,0.253906],[-0.025,0.100625],[0.0125,-0.049844],[0.05,-0.1975],[0.0875,-0.342344],[0.125,-0.484375],[0.1625,-0.623594],[0.2,-0.76],[0.2375,-0.893594],[0.275,-1.024375],[0.3125,-1.152344],[0.35,-1.2775],[0.3875,-1.399844],[0.425,-1.519375],[0.4625,-1.636094],[0.5,-1.75],[0.5375,-1.861094],[0.575,-1.969375],[0.6125,-2.074844],[0.65,-2.1775],[0.6875,-2.277344],[0.725,-2.374375],[0.7625,-2.468594],[0.8,-2.56],[0.8375,-2.648594],[0.875,-2.734375],[0.9125,-2.817344],[0.95,-2.8975],[0.9875,-2.974844],[1.025,-3.049375],[1.0625,-3.121094],[1.1,-3.19],[1.1375,-3.256094],[1.175,-3.319375],[1.2125,-3.379844],[1.25,-3.4375],[1.2875,-3.492344],[1.325,-3.544375],[1.3625,-3.593594],[1.4,-3.64],[1.4375,-3.683594],[1.475,-3.724375],[1.5125,-3.762344],[1.55,-3.7975],[1.5875,-3.829844],[1.625,-3.859375],[1.662