{"id":"gma-fig-055","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":1,"q":"The graph shows temperature and hot drinks sold at a kiosk on 10 days, with a line of best fit. Use the line to estimate the temperature on a day when 50 hot drinks are sold.","o":["10 °C","50 °C","8 °C","12 °C"],"a":2,"x":"Find 50 on the vertical axis, go across to the line of best fit, then down to the temperature axis. The line gives 8 °C. Use the line, not a single plotted point.","w":["Used the plotted point at 50 drinks (10 °C) instead of the line of best fit.","Gave the number of drinks as the temperature, mixing up the two axes.","","Read the line at 40 drinks instead of 50 on the vertical axis."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,20],"y":[0,80],"xstep":4,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"temperature / °C","ylabel":"hot drinks sold","lines":[{"pts":[[2,68],[4,62],[6,52],[8,48],[10,50],[12,40],[14,36],[16,28],[18,26],[20,18]],"dots":true,"c":0},{"pts":[[0,70],[20,20]],"c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-056","s":"maths","t":"S6","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows plant height and age, with a line of best fit. The dashed part extends the line. Use the line to estimate the height at 20 days. Which statement is correct?","o":["50 cm; reliable, because the points lie close to the line","50 cm; unreliable, because 20 days is outside the data range","30 cm; reliable, because the line passes through the data","40 cm; unreliable, because 20 days is outside the data range"],"a":1,"x":"At 20 days the extended line reaches 50 cm. The plotted data only covers about 2 to 10 days. So this is extrapolation, and the trend may not continue. The estimate is unreliable.","w":["Ignored that 20 days is outside the data. Points close to the line do not make extrapolation reliable.","","Read the line at 10 days, the end of the data, instead of at 20 days, then called it reliable.","Read the line at 15 days (40 cm) instead of 20 days. The comment on reliability is right."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,24],"y":[0,60],"xstep":4,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"age / days","ylabel":"height / cm","lines":[{"pts":[[2,14],[3,18],[4,17],[5,22],[6,22],[7,26],[8,25],[9,30],[10,31]],"dots":true,"c":0},{"pts":[[0,10],[10,30]],"c":1},{"pts":[[10,30],[20,50]],"dash":true,"c":2}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-036","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":2,"q":"A tank already has some water in it when a tap is turned on. Use the graph to find the rate at which the volume of water in the tank increases.","o":["10 litres per minute","0.125 litres per minute","8 litres per minute","20 litres per minute"],"a":2,"x":"Rate of change is the gradient. From the graph, the volume goes from 20 to 100 litres as time goes from 0 to 10 minutes. $(100-20)\\div10=8$ litres per minute.","w":["You divided the final volume by the time, $100\\div10$. The line does not start at zero, so you must use the change in volume.","You turned the gradient upside down and found $10\\div80$. The gradient is change in volume over change in time.","","You read where the line crosses the vertical axis. That is the starting volume, not the rate."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,12],"y":[0,120],"xstep":2,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"time / minutes","ylabel":"volume of water / litres","lines":[{"pts":[[0,20],[10,100]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[0,20],"label":"A"},{"at":[10,100],"label":"B"}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-037","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":3,"q":"The bar chart shows the surface areas of three similar solids. The medium solid is 8 cm tall. Use the chart to find the height of the large solid.","o":["12 cm","18 cm","24 cm","27 cm"],"a":0,"x":"From the chart, the medium area is 40 cm² and the large area is 90 cm². Area scale factor $90\\div40=\\frac{9}{4}$. Length scale factor $\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{4}}=\\frac{3}{2}$. Height is $8\\times\\frac{3}{2}=12$ cm.","w":["","You used the area scale factor $\\frac{9}{4}$ as the length scale factor. Lengths use the square root of the area factor.","You compared the small and large bars instead of the medium and large bars. That gives a length factor of 3.","You cubed the length scale factor, which is for volumes. Surface area is an area, so take the square root only."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Small","Medium","Large"],"vals":[10,40,90],"y":[0,90],"ystep":10,"xlabel":"solid","ylabel":"surface area / cm²"}}
{"id":"gma-fig-038","s":"maths","t":"R12-R14","lv":3,"q":"The curve shows how far a toy car has travelled over time. The dashed line is the tangent to the curve at P. Use the tangent to find the gradient at P and say what it shows.","o":["2 m/s, the speed at P","4 m/s, the speed at P","0.25 m/s, the speed at P","8 m/s, the speed at P"],"a":1,"x":"The tangent rises from 0 to 16 m as time goes from 2 to 6 s. Gradient $=16\\div4=4$. The gradient of a distance-time graph is speed, so the speed at P is 4 m/s.","w":["You divided the distance at P by the time at P. That gives the average speed from the start, not the speed at P.","","You turned the gradient upside down and found change in time over change in distance.","You read the distance at P off the vertical axis. A gradient needs change in distance over change in time."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,32],"xstep":1,"ystep":4,"xlabel":"time / s","ylabel":"distance / m","lines":[{"pts":[[0.0,0.0],[0.05,0.00125],[0.1,0.005],[0.15,0.01125],[0.2,0.02],[0.25,0.03125],[0.3,0.045],[0.35,0.06125],[0.4,0.08],[0.45,0.10125],[0.5,0.125],[0.55,0.15125],[0.6,0.18],[0.65,0.21125],[0.7,0.245],[0.75,0.28125],[0.8,0.32],[0.85,0.36125],[0.9,0.405],[0.95,0.45125],[1.0,0.5],[1.05,0.55125],[1.1,0.605],[1.15,0.66125],[1.2,0.72],[1.25,0.78125],[1.3,0.845],[1.35,0.91125],[1.4,0.98],[1.45,1.05125],[1.5,1.125],[1.55,1.20125],[1.6,1.28],[1.65,1.36125],[1.7,1.445],[1.75,1.53125],[1.8,1.62],[1.85,1.71125],[1.9,1.805],[1.95,1.90125],[2.0,2.0],[2.05,2.10125],[2.1,2.205],[2.15,2.31125],[2.2,2.42],[2.25,2.53125],[2.3,2.645],[2.35,2.76125],[2.4,2.88],[2.45,3.00125],[2.5,3.125],[2.55,3.25125],[2.6,3.38],[2.65,3.51125],[2.7,3.645],[2.75,3.78125],[2.8,3.92],[2.85,4.06125],[2.9,4.205],[2.95,4.35125],[3.0,4.5],[3.05,4.65125],[3.1,4.805],[3.15,4.96125],[3.2,5.12],[3.25,5.28125],[3.3,5.445],[3.35,5.61125],[3.4,5.78],[3.45,5.95125],[3.5,6.125],[3.55,6.30125],[3.6,6.48],[3.65,6.66125],[3.7,6.845],[3.75,7.03125],[3.8,7.22],[3.85,7.41125],[3.9,7.605],[3.