{"id":"gma-fig-044","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the number of pupils in each year group at a school. A stratified sample of 50 pupils is taken. How many pupils should be chosen from Year 9?","o":["$10$","$8$","$80$","$16$"],"a":1,"x":"Add the bars to get the school total: $140+120+80+100+60=500$. Year 9 is $\\frac{80}{500}$ of the school. $\\frac{80}{500}\\times 50=8$.","w":["Shared the sample equally between the 5 year groups ($50\\div5$) instead of using the bar heights.","","Read the height of the Year 9 bar and gave it as the sample size.","Divided the Year 9 bar by 5 (the number of year groups) instead of finding its share of the total."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Year 7","Year 8","Year 9","Year 10","Year 11"],"vals":[140,120,80,100,60],"y":[0,160],"ystep":20,"xlabel":"year group","ylabel":"number of pupils"}}
{"id":"gma-fig-045","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows quarterly sales of ice lollies. Quarter 1 is Jan–Mar of Year 1. The summer peak rises by the same amount each year. Predict the Year 3 peak, in thousands.","o":["$80$","$20$","$140$","$100$"],"a":3,"x":"The summer peaks are at quarters 3 and 7. Year 1 peak is 60 and Year 2 peak is 80. That is a rise of 20 a year, so the Year 3 peak is $80+20=100$.","w":["Gave the Year 2 peak again, so assumed there was no rise.","Gave the yearly rise from the graph instead of adding it to the Year 2 peak.","Added the two peaks, $60+80$, instead of adding the rise to the last peak.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,8],"y":[0,100],"xstep":1,"ystep":20,"xlabel":"quarter number","ylabel":"sales / thousands","lines":[{"pts":[[1,20],[2,40],[3,60],[4,30],[5,30],[6,50],[7,80],[8,40]],"c":0}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-046","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":2,"q":"The frequency polygon shows how long students take to travel to school. The classes are 0–20, 20–40, 40–60 and 60–80 minutes. How many students travel for more than 40 minutes?","o":["$16$","$10$","$22$","$14$"],"a":0,"x":"Each point is at the middle of its class. The classes over 40 minutes have midpoints 50 and 70. Their frequencies are 10 and 6. $10+6=16$.","w":["","Counted only the 40–60 class and left out the 60–80 class.","Added the classes up to 40 minutes ($8+14$) instead of the ones over 40.","Read the height at the midpoint 30, which is the 20–40 class."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,80],"y":[0,16],"xstep":10,"ystep":2,"xlabel":"time taken / minutes","ylabel":"frequency","lines":[{"pts":[[10,8],[30,14],[50,10],[70,6]],"c":0}],"points":[{"at":[10,8],"label":""},{"at":[30,14],"label":""},{"at":[50,10],"label":""},{"at":[70,6],"label":""}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
{"id":"gma-fig-047","s":"maths","t":"S1-S2","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the favourite lunch of a random sample of pupils. The school has 900 pupils. Use the sample to estimate how many pupils in the school would choose pizza.","o":["$24$","$15$","$360$","$540$"],"a":2,"x":"Add the bars to find the sample size: $24+12+8+16=60$. Pizza is $\\frac{24}{60}=0.4$ of the sample. $0.4\\times900=360$.","w":["Read the pizza bar and gave the sample count, not an estimate for the whole school.","Worked out $900\\div60$ but did not multiply by the pizza bar.","","Used the pupils who did not choose pizza ($\\frac{36}{60}\\times900$)."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["Pizza","Pasta","Salad","Sandwich"],"vals":[24,12,8,16],"y":[0,28],"ystep":4,"xlabel":"favourite lunch","ylabel":"number of pupils"}}
{"id":"gma-fig-052","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":2,"q":"The graph shows the number of pets owned by each student in class A. Class B has a mean of 2.4 pets and a range of 4 pets. Which statement is correct?","o":["Class A has more pets on average and the same range","Class A has fewer pets on average and a wider range","Class A has fewer pets on average and the same range","Class A has fewer pets on average and a smaller range"],"a":2,"x":"Total pets $=0\\times3+1\\times6+2\\times5+3\\times4+4\\times2=36$. Mean $=36\\div20=1.8$, which is less than 2.4. Range $=4-0=4$, the same as class B.","w":["Uses the bar heights as the values, so the typical value looks big. The mean must use pets times frequency.","Counts the 5 bars as the range. The range is the largest value minus the smallest value on the horizontal axis.","","Finds the range from the bar heights of the first and last bars (3 − 2 = 1) instead of from the values on the horizontal axis."],"fig":{"type":"bar","cats":["0","1","2","3","4"],"vals":[3,6,5,4,2],"y":[0,8],"ystep":1,"xlabel":"number of pets","ylabel":"frequency"}}
{"id":"gma-fig-053","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows box plots of journey times to school for group A and group B. How much bigger is the interquartile range of A than the interquartile range of B?","o":["$10$ minutes","$15$ minutes","$5$ minutes","$25$ minutes"],"a":1,"x":"A: lower quartile 10, upper quartile 35, so IQR $=35-10=25$. B: lower quartile 20, upper quartile 30, so IQR $=30-20=10$. Difference $=25-10=15$ minutes.","w":["Takes the difference between the ranges (35 − 25) instead of the interquartile ranges.","","Takes the difference between the medians (25 − 20) instead of the interquartile ranges.","Finds the IQR of A only and forgets to subtract the IQR of B."],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,45],"y":[0,6],"xstep":5,"ystep":1,"xlabel":"journey time / minutes","ylabel":"group","lines":[{"pts":[[5,4.5],[10,4.5],[10,5],[35,5],[35,4],[10,4],[10,5],[20,5],[20,4],[35,4],[35,4.5],[40,4.5]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[10,1.5],[20,1.5],[20,2],[30,2],[30,1],[20,1],[20,2],[25,2],[25,1],[30,1],[30,1.5],[35,1.5]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":false}}
{"id":"gma-fig-054","s":"maths","t":"S4-S5","lv":3,"q":"The graph shows cumulative frequency curves for waiting times at clinics A and B. Each clinic had 80 patients. Which statement is correct?","o":["B has the lower median wait, and B's waits are more consistent","A has the lower median wait, and A's waits are more consistent","B has the lower median wait, but A's waits are more consistent","A has the lower median wait, but B's waits are more consistent"],"a":3,"x":"Read at cumulative frequency 20, 40 and 60. A: median 20, IQR $=40-10=30$. B: median 30, IQR $=40-20=20$. A has the lower median. B has the smaller IQR, so B is more consistent.","w":["Thinks the lower curve has the lower median, so swaps the curves for the median. The IQR is read correctly.","Reads the median correctly but thinks a bigger IQR means more consistent. A smaller IQR is more consistent.","Swaps the two curves throughout, so both the median and the spread are attached to the wrong clinic.",""],"fig":{"type":"graph","x":[0,70],"y":[0,80],"xstep":10,"ystep":10,"xlabel":"waiting time / minutes","ylabel":"cumulative frequency","lines":[{"pts":[[0,0],[10,20],[20,40],[30,50],[40,60],[50,72],[60,80]],"label":"A","c":0},{"pts":[[0,0],[10,5],[20,20],[30,40],[40,60],[50,70],[60,76],[70,80]],"label":"B","c":1}],"xnums":true,"ynums":true}}
